/** Prepared CRS explanations; Bible references included. No live API needed. */
(window.TEACH_ME = window.TEACH_ME || {}).crs = {
 "|According to Genesis, on which day did God create man?|Fourth day|Fifth day|Sixth day|Seventh day": "Human beings are created on the sixth day, after the land animals, and the completed creation is described as very good. God's rest belongs to the seventh day. The trap: creation of humans and the day of rest are consecutive events, not the same day.\n\nReference: Genesis 1:26–31.",
 "|The covenant God made with Abraham included the sign of:|Sabbath observance|Circumcision|Animal sacrifice|The rainbow": "God identifies circumcision as the sign of his covenant with Abraham and his descendants. The rainbow belongs to the covenant with Noah, while Sabbath observance is associated with Israel's covenant life. Keep the covenant's participants and the sign together rather than choosing any familiar religious practice.\n\nReference: Genesis 17:9–14.",
 "|In the common Protestant numbering of the Ten Commandments, which commandment deals with the Sabbath?|Second commandment|Third commandment|Fourth commandment|Fifth commandment": "The command is to remember the Sabbath and keep it holy. Common Protestant lists call it the fourth commandment; Catholic and Lutheran lists commonly call it the third because they group the commands differently. This question now specifies the numbering tradition so that a student is not penalised for another valid arrangement.\n\nReference: Exodus 20:8–11.",
 "|In the New Testament, Jesus' first public miracle was:|Feeding the five thousand|Raising Lazarus|Turning water into wine at Cana|Healing a blind man": "At Cana, Jesus turns water into wine and John calls this the beginning of his signs, revealing his glory and leading his disciples to believe. Raising Lazarus happens much later. The sign's place in John's narrative, rather than how spectacular it seems, establishes its position as first.\n\nReference: John 2:1–11.",
 "|The Sermon on the Mount is recorded mainly in:|Mark 5-7|Luke 6-8|Matthew 5-7|John 5-7": "Matthew's extended Sermon on the Mount occupies chapters 5–7, beginning with the Beatitudes and ending with the two builders. Luke contains related teaching, but the alternatives do not identify the collection conventionally called the Sermon on the Mount. Learn the Gospel and chapter range together.\n\nReference: Matthew 5–7.",
 "|Paul's letter to the Romans was written to:|Correct moral problems in the church at Rome|Urge the collection of money for Jerusalem|Introduce himself and explain the gospel systematically|Address disputes about speaking in tongues": "Paul writes to believers he hopes to visit, explains the gospel and describes plans for further mission towards Spain. Romans therefore serves both theological exposition and an introduction to his intended ministry among them. Collections and particular church disputes are more immediate concerns in some other letters.\n\nReference: Romans 1:8–17; 15:22–24.",
 "|Which of the following prophets is often called 'the father of writing prophets' in Hebrew history?|Jonah|Amos|Joel|Obadiah": "Amos is conventionally called an early writing prophet, associated with the eighth century BC and vigorous preaching about justice. “Father of writing prophets” is a school-description label, not a biblical title. Proposed dates of other prophetic books differ, so avoid turning the label into an absolute chronology claim.\n\nReference: Amos 1:1.",
 "|The concept of Shema as found in Deuteronomy 6:4 begins with:|'Love your neighbour as yourself'|'Hear, O Israel: The LORD our God, the LORD is one'|'You shall have no other gods before me'|'Remember the Sabbath day'": "Shema means “Hear,” from the opening command to Israel. The declaration identifies the LORD as Israel's God and affirms his oneness; the call to love him with heart, soul and might follows. Loving one's neighbour is another biblical command, but it is not the opening of this passage.\n\nReference: Deuteronomy 6:4–5.",
 "|According to Luke's Gospel, what was the message of the angels to the shepherds at Jesus' birth?|'He will save his people from their sins'|'This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased'|'Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, goodwill toward men'|'Behold the Lamb of God'": "Luke places the angelic praise after the announcement of the Saviour's birth to the shepherds. The saying about the beloved Son belongs to Jesus' baptism or transfiguration, and “Lamb of God” to John's testimony. Match the speaker and event before choosing a familiar quotation.\n\nReference: Luke 2:8–14.",
 "|The 'Suffering Servant' passages are found in the book of:|Psalms|Isaiah|Ezekiel|Jeremiah": "Isaiah's servant material describes rejection, suffering and vindication, including suffering associated with others' wrongdoing. Christian New Testament interpretation applies this to Jesus. The question asks for the Old Testament book containing these passages, not the later books that quote or interpret them.\n\nReference: Isaiah 52:13–53:12.",
 "|According to Matthew 6, when Jesus taught on prayer, he warned against:|Praying in public|Using too few words|Vain repetition and praying to be seen by men|Praying at night": "Jesus criticises praying for public admiration and empty, multiplying words. He does not prohibit every prayer heard by other people or establish a rule that every prayer must be short. The problem is ostentation and treating words as a technique for forcing God's attention.\n\nReference: Matthew 6:5–8.",
 "|The Book of Acts is the continuation of:|The Gospel of Mark|The Gospel of John|The Gospel of Luke|The Gospel of Matthew": "Acts refers to the writer's earlier account of what Jesus began to do and teach, and addresses Theophilus as Luke does. The link is literary and historical: the first volume narrates Jesus' ministry and the second the witness of his followers through the Spirit.\n\nReference: Luke 1:1–4; Acts 1:1–2.",
 "|At the Council of Jerusalem (Acts 15), the apostles met to resolve the issue of:|Whether Gentile converts needed to be circumcised and keep the Mosaic Law|The appointment of deacons|The collection for the poor in Jerusalem|Paul's missionary journeys": "The central dispute was whether Gentile believers needed circumcision and observance of the Mosaic law to be saved. The council rejected imposing that requirement and issued a letter with specific directions. Appointing the Seven for community service is an earlier Acts episode, not the council's main issue.\n\nReference: Acts 15:1–29.",
 "|In the parable of the Prodigal Son, when the son returned, the father:|Punished him first before welcoming him|Ignored him|Ran to meet him, embraced him and threw a celebration|Sent him to work as a servant": "The father sees the returning son, runs, embraces him and orders a celebration because the lost son has been found. The story depicts a welcome greater than the son's proposed status as a hired worker. It teaches mercy and restoration, not approval of the waste that led him away.\n\nReference: Luke 15:20–24.",
 "|Which of the following best describes Paul's teaching on justification by faith in Galatians?|Humans are saved by strict obedience to the law|Faith in Christ alone justifies a person before God, not works of the law|Salvation is achieved by a combination of faith and circumcision|The law must be observed for initial salvation": "Paul repeatedly contrasts justification through faith in Christ with works of the law. Requiring circumcision as a condition of salvation would undermine the sufficiency of Christ. This does not make moral conduct unimportant; it distinguishes the basis of acceptance before God from the conduct that should follow faith.\n\nReference: Galatians 2:16.",
 "|The transfiguration of Jesus is recorded in all of the following EXCEPT:|Matthew 17|Mark 9|Luke 9|John 12": "The transfiguration appears in Matthew, Mark and Luke, the Synoptic Gospels. John 12 contains different events and teaching, not this mountain scene with Moses and Elijah. The word EXCEPT asks for the option that does not belong, even though the other three chapter references are correct.\n\nReference: Matthew 17:1–8; Mark 9:2–8; Luke 9:28–36.",
 "|In the Old Testament, who was the first person described as 'filled with the Spirit of God' for a specific task?|Moses|Bezalel|Samuel|David": "God says that he has filled Bezalel with the Spirit of God for skilled work on the sanctuary. The question concerns the explicit description of being filled for a particular task, not the first biblical person with whom God's Spirit is associated in any wording. His gift serves practical craftsmanship.\n\nReference: Exodus 31:1–5.",
 "|Jesus described himself as 'the bread of life' in which Gospel?|Matthew 6|Mark 8|Luke 9|John 6": "Jesus identifies himself as the bread of life in John 6, after the feeding sign and the crowd's search for bread. His teaching directs them beyond perishable food to life through him. This “I am” identification is not located in the other chapter references given.\n\nReference: John 6:35,48.",
 "|Which of the following events in the life of Jesus fulfilled Isaiah's prophecy about a virgin birth?|The Baptism of Jesus|The birth of Jesus from the Virgin Mary|The Resurrection|The Ascension": "Matthew applies the Immanuel prophecy to Jesus' birth from Mary. The Christian interpretation concerns his birth, not baptism or resurrection. Distinguish the Old Testament wording and its historical setting from the New Testament author's explicit application to Jesus; both belong to understanding the reference.\n\nReference: Isaiah 7:14; Matthew 1:22–23.",
 "|The epistles of 1 and 2 Thessalonians deal primarily with:|Church order and the role of women|Correct doctrine about the Second Coming of Christ|The nature of Christ's divinity|Jewish-Gentile relations in the church": "Both letters address believers' hope and confusion about Christ's coming. The first comforts them about those who have died and calls for watchfulness; the second warns against claims that the day has already arrived. Neither encourages date-setting or abandoning ordinary responsibilities.\n\nReference: 1 Thessalonians 4:13–5:11; 2 Thessalonians 2:1–3.",
 "|According to Acts 2, what was the immediate evidence of the Holy Spirit's coming at Pentecost?|A great earthquake|The disciples speaking in other tongues as the Spirit enabled them|The blinding of religious leaders|A pillar of cloud": "The Spirit's coming is described with a sound like a mighty wind, tongues as of fire and speech in other tongues. The option gives the disciples' immediate Spirit-enabled activity. The account does not describe an earthquake or the blinding of religious leaders at Pentecost.\n\nReference: Acts 2:1–4.",
 "|The Book of Revelation was written by:|Paul|Peter|John the Apostle (exiled on Patmos)|Luke": "Revelation identifies its writer as John, who says he was on Patmos because of God's word and Jesus' testimony. Christian tradition often identifies him with the apostle. The text's self-identification and the traditional further identification should be distinguished rather than claiming the book explicitly supplies every biographical detail.\n\nReference: Revelation 1:1,9.",
 "|What was the purpose of Jesus' baptism according to Matthew 3?|To acknowledge his sin|To 'fulfil all righteousness' and identify with humanity|To receive power for miracles|To be appointed as high priest": "Jesus tells John to permit the baptism because it is fitting to fulfil all righteousness. The narrative does not present him as confessing personal sin. The descent of the Spirit and the heavenly voice follow, but they are not substitutes for Jesus' stated reason in the exchange.\n\nReference: Matthew 3:13–17.",
 "|In the Old Testament, the concept of 'hesed' (often translated 'lovingkindness' or 'steadfast love') primarily describes:|God's holiness and separation from sin|God's loyal, covenant love and mercy toward Israel|The wrath of God against disobedience|The purity required for worship": "Hesed describes steadfast, loyal love and mercy in covenant relationships. It is not simply emotional affection or a synonym for judgement. In Hosea, God's loyalty stands against Israel's unfaithfulness, showing that covenant love carries commitment and calls for a faithful response.\n\nReference: Exodus 34:6–7; Hosea 2:19–20.",
 "|Which patriarch receives extended attention as an example of faith in Hebrews 11?|Moses alone|Abraham is given primary emphasis|David|Elijah": "Abraham receives extended attention for obeying God's call, trusting the promise and facing the test concerning Isaac. Hebrews also honours Sarah, Moses and many others. It does not formally rank one person as the supreme example; this corrected stem asks specifically for the patriarch receiving this sustained treatment.\n\nReference: Hebrews 11:8–19.",
 "|The concept of 'theocracy' as a form of government is best illustrated in Israel by:|The period of the United Monarchy under David|The period of the Judges when God was considered Israel's direct ruler|The Babylonian Exile|The Maccabean revolt": "Theocracy means government under God's authority. In accounts of the judges, human leaders are raised to deliver the people, while the LORD is understood as Israel's ruler. Israel's later request for a king exposes the tension. Theocracy does not mean there were no human officials or decisions.\n\nReference: Judges 2:16–19; 1 Samuel 8:4–9.",
 "|Paul's letter to Philemon is primarily about:|Theological arguments about the law|Requesting Philemon to receive back Onesimus, a runaway slave, as a brother in Christ|Instructions on church leadership|The collection for the saints": "Paul appeals for Onesimus as his spiritual child and urges Philemon to receive him as a beloved brother. The letter is a personal request shaped by Christian fellowship, not a systematic argument about circumcision. It calls for a transformed relationship rather than treating conversion as irrelevant to practical conduct.\n\nReference: Philemon 10–17.",
 "|In the Gospel of John, the raising of Lazarus serves primarily as:|A demonstration of Jesus' compassion for grief-stricken families|The seventh and climactic 'sign' pointing to Jesus as the resurrection and the life|Proof that the dead can communicate with the living|A contradiction of the Pharisees' teaching on resurrection": "The raising of Lazarus reveals Jesus as the resurrection and the life and intensifies opposition to him. Many teaching outlines arrange seven signs and place this miracle seventh. That classification is useful, but the Gospel does not itself attach a numbered “seventh sign” label to the event.\n\nReference: John 11:1–44.",
 "|Which of the following best describes the Pharisees in New Testament times?|Sadducees who rejected oral tradition|A Jewish sect committed to strict observance of the written and oral Law (Torah)|Roman sympathisers who controlled the Temple|Priests who officiated in Jerusalem's Temple": "The Pharisees emphasised observance of the law and ancestral tradition and, unlike the Sadducees, affirmed resurrection. They should not be reduced to all priests or all Roman collaborators. The question tests the group's characteristic commitments, not a judgement that every individual Pharisee behaved identically.\n\nReference: Mark 7:1–13; Acts 23:6–8.",
 "|According to 1 Corinthians 13, love (agape) is described as:|A feeling of affection that depends on circumstances|The greatest of all spiritual gifts|The foundation of all charismatic gifts|Patient, kind, not envious, not self-seeking — and the greatest of faith, hope and love": "Paul describes love through conduct: patience, kindness, refusal of envy and selfishness, and endurance. He concludes that love is greatest among faith, hope and love. This is more precise than calling love merely a feeling or treating it as another spectacular ability to display competitively.\n\nReference: 1 Corinthians 13:4–13.",
 "|The figure of the 'Suffering Servant' who 'bore our griefs and carried our sorrows' (Isaiah 53) is most directly applied in the New Testament to:|Moses' intercession for Israel|The suffering of the early church under persecution|Jesus' atoning death and resurrection|The exile of Judah to Babylon": "Christian New Testament interpretation applies Isaiah's suffering-servant language to Jesus, particularly his bearing sins in his death. In Acts, Philip begins from this passage to explain Jesus to the Ethiopian official. The connection is therefore more specific than suffering in general or the exile considered on its own.\n\nReference: Isaiah 53:4–12; Acts 8:30–35; 1 Peter 2:24.",
 "|The Nicene Creed (325 AD) was formulated primarily to address the heresy of:|Gnosticism|Arianism — the teaching that the Son was created and not co-eternal with the Father|Pelagianism — salvation by works|Donatism — validity of sacraments depending on the minister's holiness": "The council addressed the controversy associated with Arius over the Son's relationship to the Father and affirmed the Son's full divinity. This is later church history, not the Jerusalem Council in Acts. Pelagian and Donatist disputes concern different questions and should not be substituted for the issue at Nicaea.\n\nReference: Council of Nicaea, AD 325.",
 "|In the Old Testament, the primary role of the prophet was:|To predict future events exclusively|To speak God's Word authoritatively to the people — calling them back to covenant faithfulness|To perform miracles|To officiate at Temple sacrifices": "Prophets speak God's message, often confronting covenant unfaithfulness and injustice. Foretelling events can be part of that role, but it is not its entire meaning. Amos' call for justice and Jeremiah's commission show that prophetic work addresses present conduct as well as future consequences.\n\nReference: Amos 5:21–24; Jeremiah 1:4–10.",
 "|According to the Synoptic Gospels, Jesus’ Last Supper was a:|Passover meal|Feast of Tabernacles meal|Day of Atonement meal|Pentecost meal": "The Synoptic narratives present the Last Supper as a Passover meal. The former question mixed John's preparation-for-Passover chronology with an imprecise Synoptic date statement, so its stem and options have been repaired. Answer from the named Gospel accounts instead of combining them into a false single timetable.\n\nReference: Mark 14:12–17; Luke 22:7–15.",
 "|The concept of 'Imago Dei' (image of God) in Genesis 1:26-27 means:|Humans look physically like God|Humans are made to reflect and represent God in creation — bearing qualities of reason, creativity, morality and relationality|Every human being is divine|Only priests bear God's image": "The image of God concerns human likeness, representation and responsibility within creation. It supports dignity and accountability rather than the idea that people are physically identical to God or themselves divine. Priests are not the only people described as bearing that image.\n\nReference: Genesis 1:26–28.",
 "|The Book of Job primarily deals with:|The rewards for righteous living|The meaning of undeserved suffering and the sovereignty of God|The prophecy of Israel's restoration|The law of Moses and its application": "Job challenges the assumption that suffering automatically proves personal wrongdoing. The reader learns of his integrity before the friends' accusations, and God's later speeches expose human limits. The book is not a simple guarantee of comfort for good people or permission to blame every sufferer.\n\nReference: Job 1–2; 38–42.",
 "|Which of the following characterises the prophetic message of Hosea?|God's judgment on Babylon|God's faithful, covenant love (hesed) for Israel despite her unfaithfulness — using the metaphor of a husband and unfaithful wife|The need for ritual purification|God's call for military strength": "Hosea uses the marriage relationship to portray Israel's unfaithfulness and God's covenant love. Idolatry is described as adultery, and repentance involves returning to the LORD and abandoning false sources of security. The metaphor should not be flattened into a lesson about ritual cleanliness alone.\n\nReference: Hosea 1–3; 14:1–9.",
 "|According to Acts, who was the first Christian martyr described in the early Church?|James the son of Zebedee|Stephen|Peter|Paul": "Stephen is the first Christian martyr described in Acts and belonged to the Seven appointed for service. He was not one of the Twelve. James son of Zebedee is the first of the Twelve whose martyrdom Acts records. The repaired stem separates those two different “first” questions.\n\nReference: Acts 6:5; 7:54–60; 12:2.",
 "|The 'Great Commission' of Matthew 28:18-20 commands disciples to:|Perform miracles in all nations|Go and make disciples of all nations, baptising them and teaching them to obey everything Christ commanded|Build churches in Jerusalem first|Preach only to Israel": "The risen Jesus commands disciple-making among all nations, with baptism and teaching obedience to his commands. The scope is not restricted to Israel, and miracle-working is not the whole commission. Teaching is linked to lived obedience rather than information without practice.\n\nReference: Matthew 28:18–20.",
 "|In the context of the Psalms, a 'lament psalm' is one in which:|The psalmist gives thanks for a victory|The psalmist cries out to God in distress, expressing pain, confusion, or complaint while maintaining trust in God|The psalmist praises God's creation|The psalmist describes the ideal king": "Lament brings distress, questions and petition before God, often with trust. Many lament psalms move towards praise, but Psalm 88 retains a dark ending. A useful pattern is not an exceptionless rule. The defining feature is prayer from suffering rather than a victory celebration or description of creation.\n\nReference: Psalms 22; 42; 88.",
 "|The book of Psalms in the Old Testament is attributed mainly to:|Solomon|David|Moses|Ezra": "David has the largest named group of psalms attributed to him, not a numerical majority of all 150. Headings also name Asaph, the sons of Korah, Solomon and Moses, while many psalms are unnamed. Traditional association with David should not erase that variety.\n\nReference: Psalm headings, including Psalms 3; 50; 72; 90.",
 "|In the New Testament, the term 'Paraclete' (used by Jesus in John's Gospel) refers to:|The Devil|The Holy Spirit as Helper, Comforter, and Advocate|The Jewish High Priest|The Roman Governor": "In these farewell teachings, Jesus promises the Helper or Advocate, identified as the Holy Spirit. Different English translations use Comforter, Counsellor or Helper for Paraclete. The term here does not refer to a Roman official or the Temple's high priest.\n\nReference: John 14:16–17,26; 15:26; 16:7.",
 "|The Council of Jerusalem (Acts 15) was convened primarily to resolve the question of:|Whether to build a temple in Antioch|Whether Gentile converts to Christianity were required to be circumcised and follow the Mosaic Law|The leadership of the early church|The date of celebrating Passover": "The Jerusalem Council responds to teaching that Gentile believers must be circumcised to be saved. Its decision refuses that requirement and issues a letter about particular practices. This is an apostolic meeting narrated in Acts, not a later church council debating the date of a festival.\n\nReference: Acts 15:1–29.",
 "|Paul's letter to the Romans is considered his most systematic theological work. Its central theme is:|Church organisation and discipline|Justification by faith — that righteousness before God comes through faith in Christ, not law-keeping|The second coming of Christ|Instructions for worship": "Paul develops the good news of righteousness received through faith in Christ and freely given grace, not earned by works of the law. Romans also addresses new life, Israel and practical conduct. Calling justification central does not mean the letter contains no other connected themes.\n\nReference: Romans 1:16–17; 3:21–28.",
 "|The Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 5-7) begins with the Beatitudes. Which of these is NOT one of the Beatitudes?|Blessed are the meek|Blessed are the peacemakers|Blessed are the wise|Blessed are the pure in heart": "The Beatitudes name the poor in spirit, mourners, meek, those hungering for righteousness, merciful, pure in heart, peacemakers and persecuted. “Blessed are the wise” is not among these openings. The question's NOT requires identifying the absent phrase, not choosing a statement that sounds religiously positive.\n\nReference: Matthew 5:3–12.",
 "|In the Old Testament, the prophet Elijah called down fire from heaven in a contest with the prophets of:|Dagon|Molech|Baal|Asherah": "At Carmel, Elijah challenges the prophets of Baal and calls on the LORD, who answers with fire. The account concerns a public choice between competing worship, not the stories of Dagon or Molech. Dagon belongs to the Philistine ark narrative; Molech to prohibited child sacrifice.\n\nReference: 1 Kings 18:20–40.",
 "|The Pentecost event described in Acts 2 marked:|The birth of Jesus|The crucifixion of Jesus|The coming of the Holy Spirit on the disciples, seen as the birth of the Church|The conversion of Paul": "Pentecost records the Spirit's coming and empowered public witness, followed by many accepting the message and baptism. Christians commonly describe this as the Church's birth. It is neither Jesus' physical birth nor Paul's later conversion, and the teaching label should not replace the events Acts actually reports.\n\nReference: Acts 2:1–4,37–41.",
 "|The covenant God made with Abraham included a promise of:|A great throne in Jerusalem|Descendants as numerous as stars, land of Canaan, and that all nations would be blessed through him|A written law on tablets|Victory over all enemies in battle": "The promises to Abraham include numerous descendants, land and blessing extending beyond his own household to other nations. A written law on Sinai and a throne in Jerusalem belong to other stages of Israel's history. Keep the patriarchal promises distinct from later institutions.\n\nReference: Genesis 12:1–3; 15:5–21; 17:1–8.",
 "|Which of the following best describes the theological significance of the Incarnation?|God punishing humanity for sin|God becoming human in Jesus Christ — fully divine and fully human — to redeem humanity from sin|The church being established by the apostles|The Holy Spirit descending at baptism": "Incarnation means the Son taking human nature in Jesus Christ. In mainstream Christian teaching he is fully divine and fully human, not merely wearing a human disguise. The Holy Spirit's descent at baptism and the formation of the Church are distinct events, not definitions of incarnation.\n\nReference: John 1:1–14; Philippians 2:5–8.",
 "|In Galatians, Paul opposes those who insisted on circumcision by arguing:|Circumcision has no meaning at all|Justification comes by faith in Christ alone; requiring circumcision undermines grace and makes Christ of no effect|The Jerusalem church approved circumcision for all|Jewish law should be fully observed by all Christians": "Paul rejects making circumcision a condition of justification because this shifts reliance from Christ to law-keeping. His argument concerns imposing it as necessary for salvation, not a claim that a physical procedure can have no cultural meaning whatsoever. Faith is to work through love.\n\nReference: Galatians 2:16–21; 5:2–6.",
 "|The signs that God showed to Moses at his call were meant to|convince Pharaoh that he was the prophet of God|portray him as superior to the Egyptian magicians|enable him to perform miracles before Pharaoh|convince the people that he had been sent by God": "Moses feared that the Israelites would not believe that God had appeared to him. The signs answered that objection: they would help the people believe his commission. Although signs later confronted Pharaoh, the immediate audience in this passage is the people of Israel.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 4:1–9.",
 "|“The LORD give you children by this woman…” Eli offered this prayer because of Hannah's|loan of Samuel to the LORD|faith in God|love for the service of the LORD|desire for more children from God": "Hannah had dedicated Samuel to the LORD, fulfilling her vow. Eli therefore blessed Elkanah and Hannah, praying for more children in place of the child she had given to God's service. This is a later blessing, not Eli's first response to her prayer for a son.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 2:20–21.",
 "|“All the words which the LORD has spoken we will do.” What was Moses' response to this declaration by the Israelites?|He asked them to pick twelve stones to represent the tribes|He charged them to walk faithfully before the LORD|He offered peace offerings to the LORD|He said that the LORD was witness to their declaration": "At Sinai, Moses wrote God's words, built an altar and sent young men to offer burnt offerings and peace offerings. He then read the covenant and sprinkled its blood. The statement about the LORD being a witness belongs to Joshua's covenant renewal, not this response by Moses.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 24:3–8.",
 "|King Saul asked Jesse to send David to him because he wanted David to|kill Goliath|serve him|marry his daughter, Michal|become Jonathan's friend": "David was recommended as a skilful musician. Saul sent to Jesse for him, loved him and made him his armour-bearer; David's music brought relief when Saul was troubled. Fighting Goliath and marriage to Michal belong to later episodes, not the reason for this first summons.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 16:14–23.",
 "|King Rehoboam refused to take the counsel of the elders because|it was wrong counsel|he was afraid of the outcome of their counsel|it was a turn of affairs brought about by the LORD|he was stubborn": "The elders advised Rehoboam to serve the people, but he followed the younger men's harsh advice. The narrator explicitly explains that this turn of affairs came from the LORD to fulfil Ahijah's word. Stubbornness describes his behaviour, but the question tests the passage's stated explanation.\n\nBible reference: 1 Kings 12:6–15.",
 "|What did Saul do after his defeat of the Amalekites?|He returned to Shiloh|He set up a monument for himself|He offered sacrifices to God|He repented of his sins": "Samuel was told that Saul had gone to Carmel and set up a monument for himself before proceeding to Gilgal. Remember the order: victory, self-glorifying monument, then Samuel's confrontation. Saul's claim about keeping animals for sacrifice does not replace the action explicitly reported here.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 15:12.",
 "|“Is not David hiding himself on the hill of Hachilah, which is on the east of Jeshimon?” This report was made to King Saul by the|Philistines|Amalekites|Ziphites|Hittites": "The Ziphites informed Saul of David's hiding place near Hachilah. They had also betrayed his whereabouts in an earlier episode. The Philistines were a different group: they fought Israel and later provided David temporary refuge under Achish.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 26:1.",
 "|The sign of the covenant between God and Noah for all future generations was|a treaty of peace|the bow in the cloud|blood|circumcision": "God placed the rainbow in the clouds as the sign of his covenant with Noah, his descendants and living creatures. The promise concerned no further flood destroying all flesh. The trap: circumcision is the sign associated with Abraham's covenant, not Noah's.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 9:8–17.",
 "|“Let us flee from before Israel; for the LORD fights for them…” The Egyptians said this when|the LORD drove the sea back by a strong east wind|Moses stretched out his hand over the sea|the angel of God stood between them and the Hebrews|their chariots were clogged in the sea": "The LORD threw the Egyptian army into confusion and impeded its chariot wheels. At that point the Egyptians acknowledged that he was fighting for Israel and tried to flee. The wind and Moses' outstretched hand had helped open the way earlier.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 14:24–25.",
 "|Moses told the Israelites that the LORD would give them flesh in the evening and bread in the morning because he had|heard their prayers|seen their suffering|heard their murmurings|promised to feed them": "The people grumbled about food after leaving Egypt. Moses explained that the LORD had heard their complaints and would provide meat in the evening and bread in the morning. This question asks for the stated occasion, not the general truth that God answers prayer.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 16:6–12.",
 "|The river that flowed out of Eden divided into four rivers, namely|Pishon, Tigris, Gihon and Nile|Nile, Pishon, Euphrates and Tigris|Gihon, Pishon, Tigris and Euphrates|Pishon, Euphrates, Gihon and Jordan": "Genesis names Pishon, Gihon, Hiddekel and Euphrates. Hiddekel is the Tigris, so the option containing those four names is correct regardless of their order. Neither the Nile nor the Jordan appears as a named river in this list.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 2:10–14.",
 "|The new covenant God made with the house of Israel and the house of Judah was written on|stones|tablets|their foreheads|their hearts": "Jeremiah describes a new covenant in which God's law is placed within the people and written on their hearts. The contrast is with an external inscription: the emphasis is inward knowledge of God and forgiveness. Stone tablets belong to the Sinai covenant.\n\nBible reference: Jeremiah 31:31–34.",
 "|“When my soul fainted within me, I remembered the LORD, and my prayer came to thee…” Jonah offered this prayer|at Tarshish|at Nineveh|in the belly of the fish|in the ship": "Jonah's prayer is explicitly introduced as prayer from the fish's belly. He recalls sinking and acknowledges God's deliverance. His later preaching took place in Nineveh, but the location of this prayer is not the city or the ship.\n\nBible reference: Jonah 2:1–7.",
 "|When the Babylonians captured King Zedekiah, they|killed him immediately|put out his eyes|assaulted his wives in his presence|took his son to Babylon": "Zedekiah was brought to the Babylonian king at Riblah. His sons were killed before him; then his eyes were put out and he was bound for Babylon. Distinguish what happened to the sons from the punishment inflicted on the king himself.\n\nBible reference: 2 Kings 25:6–7.",
 "|According to Hosea, the LORD would not have pity upon the children of his faithless wife because|they had not been faithful|they were rebellious children|their mother had played the harlot|their mother had not shown pity on them": "Hosea's family imagery portrays Israel's unfaithfulness. The rejection of pity for the children is linked to their mother's harlotry and pursuit of lovers. Read this as the prophetic marriage metaphor for covenant betrayal, not an independent rule that children must always suffer for parents' sins.\n\nBible reference: Hosea 2:4–5.",
 "|The men who threw Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego into the fiery furnace were|consumed by the flames|promoted by the king|amazed that the Hebrews were not burnt|assisted to escape from being burnt": "The furnace was so fiercely heated that the flames killed the soldiers who threw the three men into it. The three Hebrews survived. The trap: the people who were killed were the executioners, not the men whom God delivered.\n\nBible reference: Daniel 3:20–23.",
 "|“Hear this word, you cows of Bashan, who oppress the poor, who crush the needy…” “Cows of Bashan” refers to the|women of Samaria|wicked kings|false prophets|judges of Israel": "Amos addresses wealthy women on the mountain of Samaria who oppress the poor and demand drink from their husbands. Bashan was known for well-fed cattle; the image rebukes luxurious living maintained through injustice. It is not a name for Israel's judges or prophets.\n\nBible reference: Amos 4:1.",
 "|God promised the faithless Israelites that, if they returned to him, he would|fill them with his knowledge|deliver their enemies into their hands|give them shepherds after his own heart|give them favour in the sight of the nations": "Jeremiah calls the faithless people to return and promises shepherds after God's own heart. These leaders will feed them with knowledge and understanding. Distinguish the gift of faithful leaders from the work those leaders are then expected to perform.\n\nBible reference: Jeremiah 3:14–15.",
 "|God decided not to bring the announced punishment during Josiah's reign because|Josiah humbled himself before the LORD and repented|he was merciful to him|Josiah walked righteously before the LORD his God|he did not want Josiah to backslide": "Huldah's message gives the reason explicitly: Josiah's heart was tender, he humbled himself, tore his clothes and wept on hearing God's words. His righteous life is relevant background, but humility in response to the discovered law is the stated reason for the reprieve.\n\nBible reference: 2 Kings 22:18–20.",
 "|Amos declared that those who built houses of hewn stone would not dwell in them because they|swore falsely|persecuted the prophets|trampled upon the poor|worshipped other gods": "Amos connects the unoccupied stone houses directly with trampling on the poor and taking grain from them. The builders' luxury came through oppression. Other sins occur in the prophets, but this verse identifies exploitation of the poor as the reason.\n\nBible reference: Amos 5:11.",
 "|What acts of righteousness did Isaiah admonish his people to demonstrate?|Helping the poor and offering sacrifices|Learning to do good and seeking justice|Defending the orphans and respecting the widows|Avoiding evil and praying for others": "Isaiah tells the people to cease evil, learn to do good and seek justice. He also names practical defence of the fatherless and the widow. The key's answer quotes one pair exactly, but the following verse also supports the option about protecting these vulnerable groups.\n\nBible reference: Isaiah 1:16–17.",
 "|“It is enough; now, O LORD, take away my life; for I am no better than my fathers.” Elijah said this after he|ran out of food|was threatened by Jezebel|learnt that Ahab was looking for him|had no water at the brook Cherith": "Jezebel threatened Elijah's life after the Carmel contest, and he fled into the wilderness. Exhausted and afraid, he prayed to die beneath a broom tree. The dried brook at Cherith belongs to an earlier stage of the drought, not this prayer.\n\nBible reference: 1 Kings 19:1–4.",
 "|“At that time shall arise … the great prince who has charge of your people…” In Daniel, this great prince is|Michael|the Deliverer|the Messiah|Gabriel": "Daniel 12 names Michael as the great prince who stands for the people. Gabriel appears elsewhere as a messenger and interpreter of visions. The question tests the named figure in this particular passage, not a general title for a deliverer.\n\nBible reference: Daniel 12:1.",
 "|While rebuilding Jerusalem's walls, Nehemiah prayed against the insults of|Nebuchadnezzar and Tobiah|Amon and Shallum|Tobiah and Sanballat|Geshem and Huldah": "Sanballat mocked the rebuilding, and Tobiah said that even a fox would break down the wall. Nehemiah responded with prayer and continued the work. Geshem also opposed him elsewhere, but Tobiah and Sanballat are the pair in this incident.\n\nBible reference: Nehemiah 4:1–5.",
 "|“The ark of the God of Israel must not remain with us…” The Philistines wanted to get rid of the ark because|their women were becoming barren|they could not handle it|the Israelites had mustered forces against them|the hand of God was heavy upon them": "After capturing the ark, the Philistines experienced devastation and tumours. They acknowledged that the hand of Israel's God was heavy upon them and upon Dagon. Their decision followed divine judgement, not a newly gathered Israelite army.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 5:6–7.",
 "|Josiah defiled Topheth in the valley of the sons of Hinnom so that|no one might sacrifice human beings again to Molech|he might pull down Topheth and break Molech into pieces|he might revive the worship of the sun god|no one would worship Molech": "The verse specifies the purpose: nobody was to make a son or daughter pass through the fire to Molech. Josiah's wider reform opposed idolatry, but this action particularly stopped child sacrifice. Choose the specific practice named in the account.\n\nBible reference: 2 Kings 23:10.",
 "|God postponed the announced disaster against Ahab's house until his son's days because Ahab|fasted for forty days|returned Naboth's vineyard|humbled himself before God|asked God for mercy": "After Elijah's judgement, Ahab tore his clothes, wore sackcloth, fasted and went about dejectedly. God expressly noted his humility and postponed the disaster. The passage does not say that Ahab restored Naboth's vineyard or fasted for forty days.\n\nBible reference: 1 Kings 21:27–29.",
 "|“Father Abraham, have mercy upon me, and send Lazarus…” Abraham could not grant the rich man's request because|his dogs used to lick Lazarus' sores|he had no pity on the rich man|there was a chasm between them|the rich man lived in luxury on earth": "Abraham explained that a great chasm had been fixed between the two places, preventing passage in either direction. The contrast between earthly luxury and suffering explains the reversal in the parable, but the chasm is the direct reason Lazarus could not cross.\n\nBible reference: Luke 16:24–26.",
 "|The Jewish religious leaders arrested and imprisoned the apostles a second time because|the high priest and the Sadducees were jealous|they disobeyed the law|they accused them of treason|they performed miracles in the name of Jesus": "Acts explicitly says that the high priest and his Sadducean party were filled with jealousy. They then arrested the apostles. Their jealousy arose amid the apostles' growing influence and signs; the miracles are background, while jealousy is the stated motive.\n\nBible reference: Acts 5:12–18.",
 "|At Jesus' transfiguration, Moses and Elijah represented the|glory of God|Old Testament saints|Law and the Prophets|end of the age": "Moses is associated with the Law and Elijah with the prophetic tradition. Their appearance with Jesus expresses continuity between his mission and the Old Testament witness. Calling them saints is broadly true, but it misses the specific Law-and-Prophets symbolism tested here.\n\nBible reference: Matthew 17:1–8; Luke 9:28–36.",
 "|“He who enters by the door is the shepherd of the sheep.” The door in Jesus' teaching refers to|Jesus|the gospel|Peter|the law": "Jesus explains the image by saying, “I am the door of the sheep.” The door signifies access through him, and he also calls himself the good shepherd later in the discourse. Do not replace his own identification with Peter or the written law.\n\nBible reference: John 10:1–9.",
 "|Jesus did not immediately go to Lazarus when he was reported sick because he|did not know how serious the sickness was|wanted his disciples to believe in him|cared less about Lazarus|was too busy attending to other people": "Jesus stayed two days and later told the disciples that, for their sake, he was glad he had not been there so that they might believe. The delay served the revelation of God's glory; it did not mean ignorance of the illness or lack of love.\n\nBible reference: John 11:4–15.",
 "|In the parable of the wheat and tares, the harvest refers to the|close of the age|millennial reign of Christ|white judgement throne|tribulation period": "In Jesus' explanation, the harvest is the close of the age and the reapers are angels. The parable concerns the final separation of evil from righteousness. Use the interpretation supplied in the passage rather than importing a different end-time timetable.\n\nBible reference: Matthew 13:36–43.",
 "|According to Luke, Jesus' last words on the cross were|“Woman, behold your son”|“Father, into thy hands I commit my spirit!”|“I thirst”|“Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do”": "Luke ends the crucifixion scene with Jesus committing his spirit to the Father and breathing his last. “I thirst” and the words to his mother occur in John. The trap: all these sayings concern the cross, but the question asks for Luke's final saying.\n\nBible reference: Luke 23:46.",
 "|To fulfil the law of Moses, Jesus told the healed leper to go and|show himself to the priest|proclaim the good news|make sacrifice to God|show himself to his household": "Jesus instructed the cleansed man to show himself to the priest and offer what Moses prescribed. Priestly examination certified cleansing and return to community life. The option about showing the priest states the first instruction precisely; general proclamation was not the command here.\n\nBible reference: Mark 1:40–44; Leviticus 14:1–32.",
 "|After the mission of the seventy, Jesus declared that he had given them|the gift of knowledge of the word|authority over all the power of the enemy|power over the Roman authorities|the gift of healing": "The seventy returned rejoicing that demons submitted to them. Jesus spoke of authority over serpents, scorpions and all the enemy's power, while directing their deeper joy to their names being written in heaven. The authority was not political control over Rome.\n\nBible reference: Luke 10:17–20.",
 "|According to Luke, Jesus healed the epileptic boy by|touching the boy|binding the evil spirit in him|rebuking the unclean spirit|telling the boy to have faith": "Luke reports that Jesus rebuked the unclean spirit, healed the boy and returned him to his father. The narrator connects the seizures with the spirit's attacks. The action asked for is the rebuke, rather than touching or asking the child himself to exercise faith.\n\nBible reference: Luke 9:37–42.",
 "|Paul warned the Thessalonians against deceptive teaching concerning|the activities of the man of sin|the second coming of Christ|their salvation in Christ|the resurrection of the dead": "Paul addresses claims that the day of the Lord had already come, in the setting of Christ's coming and believers' gathering to him. He tells the church not to be quickly shaken or deceived. The man of lawlessness appears within that discussion, not as a separate main subject.\n\nBible reference: 2 Thessalonians 2:1–3.",
 "|Believers have been buried and raised with Christ so that they might|grow in the knowledge of Christ|walk in liberty|adhere to the land|walk in newness of life": "Paul relates baptism to burial with Christ and his resurrection. The stated purpose is that believers should walk in newness of life. This is an ethical transformation: the passage does not treat participation in Christ merely as information or as permission to continue in sin.\n\nBible reference: Romans 6:3–4.",
 "|Paul teaches that individual believers' spiritual gifts should promote|cooperation|knowledge|faith|competition": "Different gifts are given for the common good, just as different parts contribute to one body. They call believers to work together and build up the church. The trap: impressive gifts do not justify rivalry, superiority or competition between members.\n\nBible reference: 1 Corinthians 12:4–27; 14:12.",
 "|Peter teaches that good Christian conduct is characterised by love and|impartial judgement|fear of God|forbearance|hard work": "Peter calls for sympathy, brotherly love, tenderness and refusal to repay evil for evil. Forbearance means patiently bearing with others rather than retaliating. Fear of God is also a Petrine instruction, but love joined with patient treatment of others best expresses the interpersonal conduct asked for.\n\nBible reference: 1 Peter 3:8–9; 4:8.",
 "|Peter teaches that Christians should prepare for the day of the Lord by|having sober reflection|giving alms to the poor|fasting and praying|living holy and godly lives": "Because the present order will pass away and God's promised future is coming, Peter asks what sort of people believers ought to be in holiness and godliness. The question tests that explicit response, rather than a list of other good religious practices.\n\nBible reference: 2 Peter 3:10–14.",
 "|According to Romans, justification is by faith through|works of righteousness|redemption in Christ|being filled with the Holy Spirit|obedience to the law": "Paul says that sinners are justified freely by God's grace through the redemption in Christ Jesus. Faith receives this gift; works of the law do not earn it. Distinguish the ground of redemption in Christ from the believer's response of faith.\n\nBible reference: Romans 3:23–28.",
 "|God sends a strong delusion upon those who perish because they|refused to believe the truth|had an unforgiving spirit|were false teachers|did not show love to the brethren": "Paul describes people who refused to love the truth and be saved, but took pleasure in unrighteousness. The delusion belongs to that judgement. The text does not identify an unforgiving attitude or the office of teacher as the specific reason in this sentence.\n\nBible reference: 2 Thessalonians 2:10–12.",
 "|Peter advises elders to tend God's flock with|zeal and credibility|authority and compassion|a willing and cheerful heart|knowledge and understanding": "Elders should oversee willingly, not under compulsion, and eagerly, not for shameful gain. They are to be examples rather than domineering rulers. The willing and cheerful heart matches these contrasts more closely than a statement about possessing authority.\n\nBible reference: 1 Peter 5:1–4.",
 "|According to Paul, the purpose of the law is|to bring justification by faith|the fulfilment of divine promises|to promote faith in the crucified Christ|to increase the knowledge of sin": "Paul writes that through the law comes knowledge of sin. The law exposes wrongdoing, but works of the law do not justify sinners before God. Do not confuse the law's diagnostic role with the saving work Paul attributes to Christ.\n\nBible reference: Romans 3:19–20; 7:7.",
 "|Because believers hold the faith of the Lord Jesus Christ, James says they should|pray for one another|not yield to temptation|overcome evil with good|not show partiality": "James opens his discussion of favouritism by telling believers not to hold faith in the glorious Lord Jesus Christ with partiality. His example contrasts treatment of rich and poor visitors. The other options may describe Christian duties, but they are not the instruction tied to this wording.\n\nBible reference: James 2:1–9.",
 "|The man who stirred up the people against Paul in Ephesus was|Aristarchus|Demetrius|Alexander|Gaius": "Demetrius, a silversmith who made shrines of Artemis, gathered fellow craftsmen because Paul's teaching threatened their trade. The resulting disturbance swept up Gaius and Aristarchus, Paul's companions. The trap: the companions seized by the crowd were not the man who started it.\n\nBible reference: Acts 19:23–29.",
 "|Paul described the gifts received from the Philippians as|a peace offering|pleasing to God|treasure in heaven|a freewill offering": "Paul compares the Philippians' support, delivered through Epaphroditus, to a fragrant offering and an acceptable sacrifice pleasing to God. The imagery expresses God's approval of their generosity; it does not identify a technical Old Testament peace offering.\n\nBible reference: Philippians 4:18.",
 "|According to Philippians, Jesus demonstrated his humility by|dying on the cross|taking the form of a servant|praying for his enemies|washing his disciples' feet": "Paul presents both taking a servant's form and obedience to death on a cross as parts of Christ's self-humbling. Verse 8 explicitly links humility with death; verse 7 supplies the servant imagery. The stem is broad enough for both aspects and should not penalise either answer.\n\nBible reference: Philippians 2:5–8.",
 "|David did not allow Abishai to destroy Saul because he|believed in the judgement of God|wanted to avoid being guilty before the LORD|wanted to disarm Saul before killing him|wanted to see whether Saul would eventually repent": "David said that striking the LORD's anointed would bring guilt, and that the LORD himself would bring Saul's life to an end. Both trusting God's judgement and avoiding guilt are explicit reasons. David did not intend to disarm Saul merely to kill him later.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 26:9–11.",
 "|The reward for total obedience to God's commandments is|wisdom|life and length of days|knowledge|material possessions": "Moses urges Israel to follow God's commands so that they may live, prosper and prolong their days in the land. The option about life and length of days follows that promise directly. Wisdom is also associated with God's instruction, but it is not the promised reward quoted here.\n\nBible reference: Deuteronomy 5:32–33; 6:1–3.",
 "|Adam named his wife Eve because she was|deceived by the serpent|the one who misled him|the bone of his bones|the mother of all living": "Genesis explains the name Eve by calling her the mother of all living. “Bone of my bones” belongs to Adam's earlier recognition of the woman in Genesis 2. The trap: two statements concern the same woman but explain different moments in the story.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 3:20.",
 "|What was Moses' immediate reaction when his rod became a serpent at Horeb?|He shouted for joy|He fled from it|He took it by the tail|He caught it immediately": "Moses first fled from the snake. Only after God instructed him did he stretch out his hand and take it by the tail, whereupon it became a rod again. The word “immediate” makes the order essential: obeying the instruction was his next action, not his first reaction.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 4:2–4.",
 "|The consequence of Solomon's failure to keep the LORD's commandment was|God's promise to tear away part of the kingdom|the disloyalty of his subjects|rebellion by vassal states|the loss of God's favour": "Because Solomon turned to other gods, the LORD announced that the kingdom would be torn away and given to his servant. For David's sake, the division would occur in Solomon's son's reign. Losing divine favour is a general description; the announced consequence is the kingdom's division.\n\nBible reference: 1 Kings 11:9–13.",
 "|According to Isaiah, God's creative ability is likened to that of a|potter and his earthen vessel|ruler and his followers|master and his servant|shepherd and his sheep": "Isaiah describes God as the potter and the people as clay or the work of his hand. The image concerns God's authority as maker and the creature's dependence on him. The shepherd image chiefly stresses guidance and care, not the shaping of a vessel.\n\nBible reference: Isaiah 45:9; 64:8.",
 "|“David departed from there and escaped … and when his brothers and all his father's house heard it…” Where did David escape to?|Mizpeh of Moab|the land of Judah|the cave of Adullam|the wilderness of Engedi": "David escaped to the cave of Adullam, where his relatives and other distressed people gathered to him. Mizpeh in Moab appears later when he sought safety for his parents. Follow the quoted opening of the episode rather than choosing another genuine refuge.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 22:1.",
 "|Hiram, king of Tyre, was friendly with Solomon because he|was afraid of war|wanted to assist Solomon|admired Solomon's wisdom|loved David, Solomon's father": "Hiram sent servants to Solomon after hearing of his accession because he had always loved David. Their later cooperation supplied materials for the Temple. The passage identifies the existing friendship with David as the reason for the first friendly approach.\n\nBible reference: 1 Kings 5:1.",
 "|“Go from your country and your kindred and your father's house…” Where was Abraham when this command was given?|Shechem|Bethel|Ur|Haran": "Genesis narrates Abram's departure for Canaan from Haran, which explains the booklet's answer. Stephen's speech in Acts, however, locates God's appearance and command in Mesopotamia before Abram lived in Haran. Without specifying the account, the question permits both settings and needs this distinction.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 11:31–12:5; Acts 7:2–4.",
 "|The LORD relented from the announced disaster against Israel because Moses|burnt the golden calf|broke the tablets|made the people drink water containing the burnt calf|reminded him of his promise to Abraham, Isaac and Israel": "Moses interceded by appealing to God's reputation and oath to the patriarchs. The LORD then relented from the disaster he had spoken of. Breaking the tablets and destroying the calf occur after this intercession, so those later actions cannot explain that earlier response.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 32:11–14.",
 "|The altar Gideon built under the oak at Ophrah was called|The LORD is present|The LORD is strength|The LORD is peace|The LORD is great": "After the angel's appearance, Gideon feared death, but the LORD reassured him with peace and told him not to fear. Gideon built an altar named “The LORD is peace.” Link the altar's name with the reassuring words immediately before it.\n\nBible reference: Judges 6:22–24.",
 "|The second creation story ends with the institution of|the Sabbath|the Passover|marriage|circumcision": "The account centred on the garden and the formation of the woman concludes with a man leaving his parents and becoming one flesh with his wife. That is the basis of marriage. The Sabbath closes the preceding creation sequence, so it is not the ending asked for here.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 2:18–25.",
 "|The domestic problem that almost cost David his kingdom was|his adultery with Bathsheba|the killing of Uriah|Adonijah's proclamation as king|Absalom's revolt against him": "Absalom gained supporters and proclaimed himself king, forcing David to flee Jerusalem. David's earlier sins brought grave consequences, but the domestic revolt that directly displaced him was Absalom's. Adonijah's attempted succession occurred much later, near the end of David's life.\n\nBible reference: 2 Samuel 15:1–18.",
 "|God's declaration that he would make a nation of the slave woman's son meant that he|recognised Ishmael as Abraham's descendant|was not partial towards Isaac and Ishmael|disliked the way Abraham sent Ishmael away|was responsible for sending Ishmael out": "God promised to make a nation of Ishmael because he too was Abraham's offspring. The covenant line was still identified with Isaac. The promise therefore recognises Ishmael's descent without saying that the two sons had identical roles in the covenant.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 21:12–13.",
 "|The chief butler's “I remember my faults today” referred to his|sins before Pharaoh imprisoned him|inability to interpret Pharaoh's dream|failure to identify competent magicians|forgetting the Hebrew who interpreted his prison dream correctly": "Joseph had asked the restored cupbearer to remember him, but the man forgot. Pharaoh's troubling dreams prompted him to recall Joseph's correct interpretation in prison. The fault relevant to the narrative is forgetting Joseph, not his inability to interpret Pharaoh's dream personally.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 40:14–23; 41:9–13.",
 "|“The earth produces of itself, first the blade, then the ear, then the full grain…” This is from the parable of the|sower|mustard seed|weeds|seed growing secretly": "In this parable a man scatters seed, sleeps and rises, while the seed grows in a way he does not understand. The stages of growth lead to harvest. The trap: the sower also involves seed, but its explanation concentrates on different soils and hearers.\n\nBible reference: Mark 4:26–29.",
 "|Peter became frightened while walking on the sea because|he saw a ghost|he doubted Jesus|he saw the wind|it was dark and cloudy": "Peter initially walked towards Jesus, but became afraid when he noticed the wind and began to sink. Jesus then reproved his little faith and doubt. The question asks what prompted his fear; doubt describes the failure exposed by that fear.\n\nBible reference: Matthew 14:28–31.",
 "|After receiving his sight near Jericho, the blind man|jumped for joy and went away|thanked Jesus for healing him|followed Jesus, glorifying God|praised God for making him whole": "Luke records that the man immediately received his sight and followed Jesus, glorifying God. The crowd also praised God. The complete option includes both the man's following Jesus and his praise, rather than praise alone or departure from Jesus.\n\nBible reference: Luke 18:35–43.",
 "|According to Luke, the order of events at the crucifixion is|crucifixion, casting lots for his clothing, scoffing and mockery|mockery, casting lots, scoffing and crucifixion|casting lots, mockery, scoffing and crucifixion|crucifixion, scoffing, mockery and casting lots": "Luke narrates the crucifixion, then the division of Jesus' clothing by lots, followed by the rulers' scoffing and the soldiers' mockery. This question asks for the order in Luke's account; it does not ask for an attempted combination of every Gospel's chronology.\n\nBible reference: Luke 23:33–37.",
 "|The angel said John the Baptist would drink no wine or strong drink and would|be the forerunner of Jesus|eat only locusts and wild honey|be filled with the Holy Spirit|baptise sinners who came to him": "The angel links John's special consecrated life with being filled with the Holy Spirit even from his mother's womb. His role as forerunner is also stated, but the promised filling with the Spirit is the description paired with abstinence in this passage.\n\nBible reference: Luke 1:13–17.",
 "|What two emblems did Jesus use at the Last Supper?|bread and the blood of the Lamb|bread and wine|unleavened bread and the fruit of the vine|his blood and his body": "The meal used bread and the cup, described as the fruit of the vine. In its Passover setting the bread was unleavened. Thus “bread and wine” and the more specific “unleavened bread and fruit of the vine” identify the same emblems; body and blood are what they signify.\n\nBible reference: Luke 22:14–20; Matthew 26:17–29.",
 "|Peter required Judas' replacement to be|a witness to Jesus' ministry|full of faith|a disciple of John the Baptist|a Galilean": "The candidate had to have accompanied Jesus' followers from John's baptism until the ascension, becoming a witness with them to the resurrection. Being merely a Galilean or a disciple of John was insufficient. The option about witnessing Jesus' ministry is the closest summary of the full requirement.\n\nBible reference: Acts 1:21–22.",
 "|“They have no wine.” Jesus' immediate response was|“Do whatever he tells you”|“Fill the jars with water”|“Draw some out and take it to the steward”|“O woman, what have you to do with me? My hour has not yet come”": "Jesus first answered his mother about his hour. Mary then told the servants to do whatever he said, and Jesus subsequently ordered the jars filled and a sample drawn. The trap: the option about obeying him was Mary's instruction, not Jesus' immediate reply.\n\nBible reference: John 2:3–8.",
 "|The Macedonians who travelled with Paul to Ephesus were|Alexander and Gaius|Demetrius and Aristarchus|Gaius and Aristarchus|Demetrius and Alexander": "Acts identifies Gaius and Aristarchus as Macedonians and Paul's travelling companions seized during the Ephesian riot. Demetrius organised the disturbance and was not one of this pair. Keep the instigator distinct from the companions caught up in the crowd.\n\nBible reference: Acts 19:29.",
 "|“O faithless and perverse generation…” Jesus’ statement was addressed to|the Jews because of their unbelief|his disciples because they could not heal|the Pharisees because of their hypocrisy|his own people because they did not give him honour": "A father reported that Jesus' disciples had been unable to expel the spirit troubling his son. Jesus' lament about the generation followed that failure. The disciples' inability supplies the immediate setting, though the rebuke addresses the wider faithless generation rather than explicitly naming only the disciples.\n\nBible reference: Luke 9:37–42; Matthew 17:14–20.",
 "|According to 1 John, to have the Son is to have|salvation|the Holy Spirit|the Father|life": "The letter states that God has given eternal life, this life is in his Son, and whoever has the Son has life. Other options may relate to Christian salvation, but “life” is the word in the particular declaration being tested.\n\nBible reference: 1 John 5:11–12.",
 "|In the explanation of the weeds, Jesus says the righteous will|live for ever in God's kingdom|be set apart from all causes of sin|enter glory with the Son of man|shine like the sun in their Father's kingdom": "After describing the removal of evil and judgement, Jesus says the righteous will shine like the sun in their Father's kingdom. The question tests that exact concluding image. General statements about eternal life do not reproduce the promised picture in this explanation.\n\nBible reference: Matthew 13:40–43.",
 "|The Jerusalem Council required Gentile believers to abstain from|adultery, greed, strangled meat and divination|homosexuality, idolatry, fornication and strangled meat|idolatry, unchastity, strangled meat and blood|circumcision, whoremongering, homosexuality and falsehood": "The council's letter names food sacrificed to idols, blood, what is strangled, and sexual immorality. It does not require circumcision for salvation. Select the option preserving all four categories, rather than substituting other behaviours for items actually listed in the letter.\n\nBible reference: Acts 15:19–20,28–29.",
 "|“Soul, you have ample goods laid up for many years…” God's response was that|the servant should enter his Lord's rest|his soul was required of him|his soul should be satisfied with his increase|he should lay up more treasure": "God called the rich man a fool and told him that his life would be required that night. His stored wealth could not guarantee the future he imagined. The lesson is against laying up treasure for oneself while failing to be rich towards God.\n\nBible reference: Luke 12:16–21.",
 "|Who first recognised Peter as one of Jesus' disciples before his three denials?|A bystander|A maid|One of the rulers|The high priest": "A servant girl noticed Peter by the fire and said that he had been with Jesus. Peter denied knowing him. Later accusers followed, but the question asks for the first recognition, so a general bystander or the high priest is not the correct identification.\n\nBible reference: Luke 22:54–57; Mark 14:66–68.",
 "|The woman with the flow of blood touched Jesus' garment because|she had heard reports about Jesus|she had suffered under many physicians|the crowd prevented her from speaking|she believed she would be made well": "She said that even touching Jesus' clothes would make her well. Hearing about him and suffering under physicians describe her background, but confidence in healing explains her action. Jesus subsequently commended her faith, connecting the result with that trust.\n\nBible reference: Mark 5:25–34.",
 "|Paul told the Thessalonians to prepare for Christ's coming by|keeping awake and being sober|praying and fasting|preaching peace and tolerance|encouraging idlers to work hard": "Paul contrasts believers, as children of light, with those who sleep in darkness. He urges watchfulness and sobriety because the day of the Lord will come unexpectedly. Sobriety here includes self-control and alertness, not merely avoiding alcoholic drink.\n\nBible reference: 1 Thessalonians 5:1–8.",
 "|While people were still God's enemies, they were reconciled to him by Christ's|suffering|death|ascension|resurrection": "Paul says that enemies were reconciled to God through the death of his Son, and then speaks of being saved by his life. Both death and resurrection matter in Paul's gospel, but the clause about reconciliation specifically names Christ's death.\n\nBible reference: Romans 5:10.",
 "|No one can say “Jesus is Lord” except by|revelation knowledge|grace|the Holy Spirit|faith": "Paul contrasts speech produced under God's Spirit with cursing Jesus, and states that confession of Jesus as Lord is by the Holy Spirit. This concerns genuine Spirit-enabled allegiance, not the physical ability of any person to pronounce the words.\n\nBible reference: 1 Corinthians 12:3.",
 "|According to Peter, the genuineness of faith is tested by|trials|fire|sufferings|temptation": "Peter describes believers grieved by various trials so that the genuineness of their faith may be proved. Fire is the comparison used for testing gold. The question asks what tests believers in the passage, so trials is the direct wording rather than the image of fire itself.\n\nBible reference: 1 Peter 1:6–7.",
 "|Paul told the Colossians not to lie because they|were joint heirs with Christ|were waiting for Christ's coming|had put off the old nature with its practices|had known the truth from the beginning": "The command not to lie follows the statement that believers have put off the old self and put on the new. Truthfulness should therefore express their changed identity. The reason here is new life in Christ, not how long someone has possessed religious knowledge.\n\nBible reference: Colossians 3:9–10.",
 "|Believers who do good before governing authorities will|receive approval|enjoy peace|be shown hospitality|be highly honoured": "Paul says that rulers should not be a terror to good conduct and that doing good brings approval. This describes the proper role of authority under God. It does not promise that every actual ruler will always treat every innocent person justly.\n\nBible reference: Romans 13:3–4.",
 "|When the chief Shepherd appears, faithful elders will receive|the unfading crown of glory|the sceptre of authority|a house of glory|a crown with stars": "After urging elders to oversee willingly and by example, Peter promises an unfading crown of glory when the chief Shepherd appears. The chief Shepherd is Christ. The reward is not a present political sceptre or a promise of material housing.\n\nBible reference: 1 Peter 5:1–4.",
 "|Paul boasted about the Corinthians for their|zeal in prayer|offering for the saints|maturity in Christ|display of spiritual gifts": "Paul told the Macedonians that Achaia had been ready to contribute, and their zeal had stirred others. The subject is the collection for needy believers, not a general assessment that the Corinthians excelled in all Christian conduct or prayer.\n\nBible reference: 2 Corinthians 9:1–2.",
 "|God exalted Jesus above every name because he|was born in human likeness|counted equality with God something to grasp|counted others better than himself|humbled himself and became obedient to death": "The hymn describes Christ's self-emptying and obedience to death on a cross, then says “therefore” God highly exalted him. Humble obedience is the link. The option claiming that he grasped at equality reverses Paul's description of the attitude he rejected.\n\nBible reference: Philippians 2:5–11.",
 "|How did Joshua react to Israel's defeat at Ai?|He tore his clothes|He fasted all day|He prayed to God|He consulted the priest": "Joshua tore his clothes and fell before the ark until evening, then addressed the LORD in distress. Tearing his clothes is the first visible response, but prayer is also plainly recorded. Since the stem does not restrict itself to the first action, both descriptions are valid.\n\nBible reference: Joshua 7:6–9.",
 "|Hosea says the upright walk in the LORD's ways, but transgressors|scorn them|reject them|stumble in them|flee from them": "Hosea's closing verse says that the LORD's ways are right: the upright walk in them and transgressors stumble in them. The contrast concerns people's response to the same right ways. Remember the paired verbs “walk” and “stumble.”\n\nBible reference: Hosea 14:9.",
 "|After Jerusalem's destruction, the Babylonians left behind the|poorest people|noblemen|priests|temple guards": "The Babylonian commander deported the remaining population but left some of the poorest people as vine-dressers and ploughmen. The nobles were not a protected group left to govern freely. The question concerns the agricultural remnant rather than those taken into exile.\n\nBible reference: 2 Kings 25:11–12.",
 "|The famine announced by Amos was a famine of|bread|water|hearing the word of the LORD|baskets of summer fruit": "God announced not a famine of bread or thirst for water, but a lack of hearing his word. People would wander seeking it without finding it. The summer-fruit basket belongs to the preceding vision, while the famine image concerns loss of access to divine guidance.\n\nBible reference: Amos 8:11–12.",
 "|“The soul that sins shall die” means that|a sinner cannot escape punishment|the wages of sin is death|God will judge the sinner's soul|every person is responsible for his own sin": "Ezekiel rejects the assumption that children must bear their parents' guilt. The son does not bear the father's iniquity, nor the father the son's: each is responsible for his own sin. The passage's specific emphasis is individual responsibility, not merely a general statement about punishment.\n\nBible reference: Ezekiel 18:4,20.",
 "|The consequence of Hiel's rebuilding Jericho was that he|was killed by Ahab|became a commander in Ahab's army|was highly respected by his tribe|lost two sons": "Hiel laid Jericho's foundation at the cost of Abiram and set up its gates at the cost of Segub. Kings connects these losses with Joshua's earlier word about rebuilding the city. The two sons' deaths, not Hiel's promotion, are the consequence being tested.\n\nBible reference: Joshua 6:26; 1 Kings 16:34.",
 "|“I send you to the people of Israel, to a nation of rebels…” This commission was given to|Isaiah|Jeremiah|Amos|Ezekiel": "God addresses Ezekiel as “son of man” and sends him to rebellious Israel, warning that the people may refuse to listen. The quoted commission identifies Ezekiel's call. Other prophets also faced opposition, but this wording belongs to his opening vision and commission.\n\nBible reference: Ezekiel 2:1–5.",
 "|Daniel's enemies who arranged the decree leading to the lions' den were the|counsellors of Nebuchadnezzar|advisers of King Xerxes|presidents and satraps of Persia|lords and nobles of the Medes": "Daniel's fellow officials could find no corruption in his work, so they targeted his prayer to God. The presidents and satraps urged Darius to prohibit petitions to anyone else. Nebuchadnezzar belongs to the earlier furnace narrative, not the king in the lions' den episode.\n\nBible reference: Daniel 6:1–9.",
 "|According to Hosea, one punishment for Israel's sins was that|they would receive no divine revelation|they would eat but not be satisfied|the LORD would send famine|their women would become barren": "Hosea declares that the people will eat but not be satisfied because they have forsaken the LORD. The verse directly matches the option about unsatisfied eating. A general famine or inability to receive revelation is not the particular punishment expressed in these words.\n\nBible reference: Hosea 4:10.",
 "|Elisha knew about Gehazi's covetous act because|it was reported to him|he saw him from afar|he went with him in spirit|he saw the gifts with him": "After Gehazi secretly obtained gifts from Naaman, Elisha asked whether his spirit had not gone with him when the man turned from his chariot. This expresses prophetic knowledge of the act. Gehazi's attempt to hide it did not depend on an informant exposing him.\n\nBible reference: 2 Kings 5:20–27.",
 "|Moses sent men to spy out Canaan in order to|measure the entire land|share out the land|know whether it was good or bad|know whether it flowed with milk and honey": "Moses instructed the spies to assess the land, people, towns, soil and produce. The words “good or bad” appear in the instructions. Their task was reconnaissance before entry, not surveying boundaries for immediate distribution or merely confirming a familiar description of fertility.\n\nBible reference: Numbers 13:17–20.",
 "|The two sons of Samuel who became judges in Israel were|Joel and Abijah|Abimelech and Abijah|Hophni and Phinehas|Joel and Abimelech": "Samuel appointed Joel and Abijah as judges at Beersheba, but they took bribes and perverted justice. The trap: Hophni and Phinehas were Eli's sons, not Samuel's. Their misconduct belongs to the earlier story of the priesthood at Shiloh.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 8:1–3.",
 "|“Let us make man in our image, after our likeness…” “In our image” implies human beings|becoming God|being God's representatives to fellow humans|being like God|becoming God's exact picture": "The image language expresses a likeness to God and the human calling to represent him in exercising responsibility over creation. It does not make humans divine or give a physical portrait of God. The option about representation is also a defensible account of the image's meaning.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 1:26–28.",
 "|The wilderness congregation's murmuring against Moses and Aaron indicated their|lack of patience with them|lack of faith in them|fear of dying by the sword|inability to reach the promised land": "The complaints expressed distrust of the leadership through which God had delivered Israel. Moses explained that the grumbling was ultimately against the LORD. Impatience may accompany a complaint, but the narratives emphasise failure to trust God's provision and promise.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 16:2–8; Numbers 14:1–4.",
 "|At the rock in the wilderness of Zin, the LORD told Moses and Aaron to|tell the rock before the people to yield its water|strike the rock with a rod|fetch water for the people and cattle|tell the people to go around the rock": "God commanded them to speak to the rock, but Moses struck it twice. Their failure to honour God before the people had consequences. The trap: striking the rock was commanded in the earlier Horeb episode, whereas speaking was commanded at this later incident.\n\nBible reference: Numbers 20:7–12.",
 "|The last event that strengthened Gideon's courage before attacking Midian was|the angel calling him a man of valour|the dew on the fleece|the test of his men at the water|the Midianite's dream about barley bread": "Gideon overheard a Midianite describe a barley loaf overturning a tent, and another interpreted it as Gideon's victory. He worshipped and returned ready to attack. The fleece and selection of the three hundred happened earlier, so they cannot be the last encouragement before the assault.\n\nBible reference: Judges 7:9–15.",
 "|At the confirmation of the covenant, the people answered Moses|“We are going to obey the LORD our God”|“The LORD is our God; we shall obey him”|“All the words which the LORD has spoken we will do”|“We shall keep all God's commandments”": "Exodus records the people answering together that they would do all the words the LORD had spoken. They repeated their commitment when Moses read the covenant book. The alternatives express similar sentiments, but this question asks for the recorded words.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 24:3–7.",
 "|Saul visited the medium at Endor when threatened by the|Philistines|Amorites|Amalekites|Moabites": "The Philistines gathered at Shunem, and Saul became afraid. Receiving no answer through dreams, Urim or prophets, he sought a medium at Endor. The Amalekite war was an earlier episode associated with his disobedience, not the enemy camped before his final battle.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 28:4–8.",
 "|Saul told the Kenites to leave the Amalekites because|God had not told him to destroy them|he had married a Kenite|they had shown kindness to Israel|they had made a blood covenant with Israel": "Saul explicitly recalled the Kenites' kindness to the Israelites when they came out of Egypt. He warned them to separate from the Amalekites before the attack. The reason is remembered kindness, not a marriage relationship or a blood covenant stated in this account.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 15:6.",
 "|“They shall be my people, and I will be their God.” This covenant was made with Israel through Prophet|Isaiah|Jeremiah|Amos|Hosea": "The quoted wording identifies Jeremiah: God promises to be the people’s God and they his people. Hosea 2:23 expresses a related covenant relationship, but in different words. A shared theme is not enough to identify the speaker of an exact quotation; B is retained.\n\nBible reference: Jeremiah 31:33; Hosea 2:23.",
 "|How many of Jesse's sons passed before Samuel before David was chosen?|Five|Six|Seven|Eight": "Jesse made seven sons pass before Samuel, but none was chosen. David, the youngest, was still tending the sheep and had to be brought in. Count the sons presented before David, not the total number including him.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 16:10–13.",
 "|“Whoever does not come out after Saul and Samuel, so shall it be done to his oxen!” Why did the people respond?|The LORD's power came upon them|The dread of the LORD fell on them|The LORD's Spirit came upon them|The LORD's word came to them": "Saul sent pieces of oxen throughout Israel as a summons, and the text says the dread of the LORD fell on the people so that they came out together. The Spirit came upon Saul earlier; do not transfer that statement to the people's response.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 11:6–7.",
 "|“Do not forsake her, and she will keep you; love her, and she will guard you.” “Her” refers to|intelligence|a wife|knowledge|wisdom": "Proverbs personifies wisdom as a woman who guards those who value her. The preceding command is to acquire wisdom and insight. The feminine pronoun therefore does not turn this into advice about choosing or keeping a human wife.\n\nBible reference: Proverbs 4:5–9.",
 "|Joseph became prime minister in Egypt because he|interpreted the dreams of Pharaoh's servants|resisted Potiphar's wife|was faithful and upright|interpreted Pharaoh's dream and advised him what to do": "Joseph explained the seven plentiful years and seven famine years and proposed collecting food under a discerning administrator. Pharaoh recognised his wisdom and appointed him. Joseph's earlier faithfulness mattered, but interpreting this dream and advising on preparation directly led to the appointment.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 41:25–43.",
 "|Nehemiah's position in exile was the king's|guard|secretary|cupbearer|adviser": "Nehemiah identifies himself as cupbearer to the king. This trusted position gave him access to Artaxerxes, whom he later asked for permission and assistance to rebuild Jerusalem. Do not confuse his court office with his later responsibilities as governor.\n\nBible reference: Nehemiah 1:11.",
 "|In Uzziah's days, Isaiah's vision concerned|Samaria and Judah|Judah and Jerusalem|Samaria and Jerusalem|Judah and Israel": "Isaiah's opening verse identifies the vision as concerning Judah and Jerusalem during the reigns of Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah. Samaria was the northern kingdom's capital. The question tests the geographical description in the book's introduction.\n\nBible reference: Isaiah 1:1.",
 "|Members of Ahab's household who died in the open country would be|eaten by dogs|buried by foreigners|eaten by birds|left unburied": "The judgement distinguishes two locations: dogs would eat those dying in the city, and birds those dying in the open country. The trap: dogs are prominent elsewhere in the Ahab and Jezebel narratives, but the location specified here calls for birds.\n\nBible reference: 1 Kings 21:24.",
 "|“Take away all iniquity; accept that which is good…” Hosea's call addresses Israel's|adultery|ignorance|idolatry|injustice": "The prayer of return goes on to renounce reliance on Assyria, horses and calling handmade objects “our God.” That context supports idolatry as the intended answer. “All iniquity” itself is broader than one sin, so the identification depends on the surrounding renunciation, not a narrow definition of the word.\n\nBible reference: Hosea 14:1–3.",
 "|The shepherds God promised through Jeremiah were to|feed the people with knowledge and understanding|heal their guilt|gather Israel's scattered children|lead them to greener pastures": "God promises shepherds after his own heart who will feed the people with knowledge and understanding. The imagery describes faithful leadership and instruction. Greener pastures belongs to a familiar shepherd image elsewhere, but it is not the task specified in this promise.\n\nBible reference: Jeremiah 3:15.",
 "|Nebuchadnezzar assembled the satraps, prefects and governors for|telling him his dream|sending them to the provinces|interpreting his dream|the dedication of the image": "The officials were summoned to the dedication of the golden image on the plain of Dura. The king's dream and its interpretation belong to a different chapter and involve wise men summoned for another purpose. Keep the golden image episode distinct from the dream of the statue.\n\nBible reference: Daniel 3:1–3.",
 "|Where did Elijah kill the prophets of Baal after the contest?|Kishon|Carmel|Zarephath|Cherith": "The contest itself occurred on Mount Carmel, but Elijah brought the captured prophets down to the brook Kishon and killed them there. The trap: the best-known place in the story is not the location of every event within it.\n\nBible reference: 1 Kings 18:40.",
 "|“The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge.” The proverb means that|parents are punished for children's sins|children are punished for parents' sins|parents are punished for grandparents' sins|great-grandchildren are punished for forefathers' sins": "The proverb expresses the complaint that children suffer the consequences of their fathers' wrongdoing. Ezekiel challenges its use to deny personal responsibility. Explaining what the proverb means is different from saying that God endorses it as the governing rule of his judgement.\n\nBible reference: Ezekiel 18:1–4.",
 "|After Jonah was thrown into the sea and the storm ceased, the sailors|praised the LORD for saving them|feared the LORD, offered sacrifice and made vows|prayed for forgiveness|rowed hard to bring the ship to land": "The sailors' final response was great fear of the LORD, sacrifice and vows. Their effort to row back to land came before they threw Jonah overboard. The narrative order separates their unsuccessful rescue attempt from their response to the suddenly calm sea.\n\nBible reference: Jonah 1:13–16.",
 "|Zedekiah was captured by the Chaldean army|at Riblah|in the plains of Jericho|at Arabah|on Jerusalem's outskirts": "The army overtook Zedekiah in the plains of Jericho and then brought him to Riblah for judgement. Riblah is where he faced the Babylonian king, not where he was captured. “Arabah” describes the wider route or region rather than the precise capture point named in the narrative.\n\nBible reference: 2 Kings 25:4–6; Jeremiah 39:4–5.",
 "|Two virtues taught by the Good Samaritan are|kindness and fairness|justice and humility|compassion and humility|compassion and mercy": "The Samaritan was moved with compassion and gave practical assistance to the wounded traveller. Jesus identified the neighbour as the one who showed mercy. Those two words are grounded directly in the story, which teaches active neighbour-love across social boundaries.\n\nBible reference: Luke 10:30–37.",
 "|“Have you come here to torment us before the time?” The Gadarene demoniacs|recognised Jesus' supreme power|wanted to question Jesus' authority|were threatened by Jesus' power|did not expect him to come then": "The speakers address Jesus as Son of God and fear the judgement he can impose. That confession recognises his authority over them. Their fear is evidence of that recognition; it is not an attempt to deny his power or to explain merely an unexpected visit.\n\nBible reference: Matthew 8:28–29.",
 "|According to Luke, Jesus commissioned the Twelve to|rid all Galilee of evil spirits|preach the gospel to the poor|preach God's kingdom and heal|go about doing good to everyone": "Jesus gave the Twelve power over demons and diseases and sent them to proclaim God's kingdom and heal. The option combines the mission's two stated activities. Other descriptions of Jesus' work are not substitutes for this particular commission.\n\nBible reference: Luke 9:1–2.",
 "|According to John, the accusation the Jews levelled against Jesus before Pilate was that he was|an evildoer|an impostor|perverted|a blasphemer": "At the first exchange with Pilate the accusers call Jesus an evildoer. Later they say that by their law he ought to die because he made himself Son of God, the basis of the blasphemy charge. Without naming the stage, the stem allows both descriptions.\n\nBible reference: John 18:29–30; 19:7.",
 "|“Go and learn what this means: I desire mercy, and not sacrifice.” What preceded these words?|Healing lepers|Eating with tax collectors|Calling the Twelve|Delivering the demoniac": "The Pharisees questioned Jesus' eating with tax collectors and sinners. He answered with the physician image and the call to learn mercy. The quotation therefore defends his fellowship with those needing repentance, not one of the listed healing incidents.\n\nBible reference: Matthew 9:10–13.",
 "|Jesus did not need to enter the centurion's house to heal his servant because the centurion|showed great faith|spoke as a man under authority|said he was unworthy for Jesus to enter|was a Gentile": "The centurion said he was unworthy to receive Jesus and trusted that a word would heal, illustrating authority by his own military experience. Jesus praised his faith. Both faith and his explicit request that Jesus need not enter explain healing at a distance; Gentile identity is not the reason.\n\nBible reference: Luke 7:6–10; Matthew 8:8–10.",
 "|The disciples prepared the Passover with Jesus because|one of them would betray him|it was the first day of Unleavened Bread|they had sacrificed the Passover lamb|they wanted to keep the feast with him": "The narrative introduces the preparation on the day of Unleavened Bread when the Passover lamb was sacrificed. The disciples asked where to prepare the meal, and Jesus sent them to the furnished room. Betrayal is discussed at the meal, not as the reason for its calendar timing.\n\nBible reference: Mark 14:12–16; Luke 22:7–13.",
 "|According to Matthew, true wisdom is|seeking first God's kingdom and righteousness|knowing the truth and doing it|bewaring of false prophets and teachers|obeying the Ten Commandments fully": "Jesus compares the hearer who acts on his words to a wise builder with a foundation on rock. The emphasis is putting received teaching into practice. “Knowing the truth and doing it” best expresses that combination, though the passage specifically concerns Jesus' words.\n\nBible reference: Matthew 7:24–27.",
 "|Jesus told Jairus, whose daughter had died, to|pray and fast|not fear, but believe|put the mourners out|repent and confess his sins": "When news came that Jairus' daughter was dead, Jesus told him not to fear but only believe. He then went on to raise her. Removing mourners occurs later and is not the reassurance or condition Jesus first addressed to the father.\n\nBible reference: Mark 5:35–36; Luke 8:49–50.",
 "|At Pentecost, Peter declared that God had made Jesus|Lord and King|holy and mighty|both Lord and Christ|victorious over death and sin": "Peter's sermon concludes that God made the crucified Jesus both Lord and Christ. Christ means Messiah, the anointed one. Other titles may be applied to Jesus elsewhere, but the question asks for the pair used at this conclusion of Peter's speech.\n\nBible reference: Acts 2:36.",
 "|The men who taught that circumcision was necessary for salvation came from|Jerusalem|Judea|Samaria|Phoenicia": "Acts introduces the dispute by saying that some men came down from Judea and taught the brothers that circumcision according to Moses was necessary for salvation. Their teaching led to the Jerusalem Council. The place of the later meeting should not replace the region specified for their arrival.\n\nBible reference: Acts 15:1.",
 "|According to John, the work of God is|preaching the gospel|healing the sick|feeding hungry people|believing in the one he has sent": "When people asked what works God required, Jesus answered that God's work was to believe in the one he sent. The answer places trust in Jesus at the centre. It does not mean that acts of mercy are bad; it identifies the response asked for in this exchange.\n\nBible reference: John 6:28–29.",
 "|“A notable sign has been performed through them…” What did the authorities eventually order these men to do?|Accept a thorough beating|Remain in prison|Never enter Samaria|Not speak in Jesus' name": "After the lame man's healing, the council acknowledged the evident sign but ordered Peter and John not to speak or teach in Jesus' name. Beating the apostles occurs in a later confrontation. This question concerns the council's response to the first healing incident.\n\nBible reference: Acts 4:16–18.",
 "|Paul was arrested at Philippi after he|continued preaching Jesus as Lord|preached in Gentile territory against the Jews' wishes|cast a spirit of divination out of a slave girl|preached about an unknown God": "Paul expelled the spirit that enabled a slave girl to earn money by divination. Her owners, seeing their profit disappear, brought Paul and Silas before the magistrates. The “unknown God” speech belongs to Athens, not the incident leading to imprisonment at Philippi.\n\nBible reference: Acts 16:16–24.",
 "|Jesus said that his disciples were already clean because of|their abiding in him|his constant abiding in them|the word he had spoken to them|their walking in the Spirit": "In the vine discourse Jesus says they are already clean because of the word he has spoken. He then urges them to abide in him. The question asks for the stated basis of their cleansing, not the closely related instruction that follows it.\n\nBible reference: John 15:3.",
 "|After Stephen's death, the believers who were not scattered from Jerusalem were the|deacons|apostles|prophets|disciples": "Acts says that persecution scattered the Jerusalem church through Judea and Samaria except for the apostles. The scattering helped spread the message, as Philip's ministry then illustrates. The exception concerns the apostles, not all disciples or everyone holding another ministry.\n\nBible reference: Acts 8:1.",
 "|The first two ministries in Paul's ordering in 1 Corinthians 12 are|healers and helpers|teachers and miracle-workers|apostles and prophets|administrators and speakers": "Paul says that God appointed first apostles, second prophets and third teachers, followed by other forms of service. He uses this ordering in his argument for different contributions to one body. Do not substitute gifts named later in the same list.\n\nBible reference: 1 Corinthians 12:28.",
 "|According to Galatians, Christ redeemed people from the curse of the law by|dying on the cross|becoming a curse for them|rising from the dead|ascending into heaven": "Paul's exact explanation is that Christ redeemed us by becoming a curse for us, citing the curse on one hung on a tree. His death on the cross is how that self-giving occurred. Both descriptions fit, although becoming a curse reproduces the explicit phrase most precisely.\n\nBible reference: Galatians 3:13.",
 "|According to James, God has promised those who love him|eternal life|abundant blessing|honourable positions|inheritance of the kingdom": "James asks whether God has chosen the poor to be rich in faith and heirs of the kingdom promised to those who love him. This rebukes discrimination against the poor. The promise in this sentence is kingdom inheritance, not worldly status or guaranteed wealth.\n\nBible reference: James 2:5.",
 "|A ruler does not bear the sword in vain, but|leads people into God's ways|executes God's wrath on the wrongdoer|rules according to popular influence|enforces civil duties": "Paul describes the authority as God's servant who bears the sword against wrongdoing. The sword represents punitive authority within the ruler's proper task. This is not a statement that every ruler's every action is morally right, or that rulers replace religious teachers.\n\nBible reference: Romans 13:3–4.",
 "|While an heir is a child, Paul says he is under|guardians|custodians|protectors|teachers": "Paul compares a minor heir, though owner of the estate, to someone under guardians and managers until the father's appointed time. He then applies the analogy to life before the coming of the Son. Guardians is the closest wording, although custodians can describe the same supervisory role.\n\nBible reference: Galatians 4:1–2.",
 "|Believers reproached for Christ's name are blessed because the Spirit of|glory and of God rests upon them|patience rests upon them|hope rests upon them|love and of God rests upon them": "Peter explicitly says that the Spirit of glory and of God rests on those insulted for Christ's name. The reason for blessedness is God's presence, not the absence of pain. Their suffering must be for Christ, rather than punishment for wrongdoing.\n\nBible reference: 1 Peter 4:14.",
 "|In relationships, Paul advises believers to|imitate his good example|obey the law of Moses|think with sober judgement|think of others first": "Paul tells Christians to imitate him as he imitates Christ, to think soberly rather than proudly, and to consider others' interests. All three are genuine instructions. Without naming a passage, this broad stem cannot distinguish the example, sober-judgement and concern-for-others options fairly.\n\nBible reference: 1 Corinthians 11:1; Romans 12:3; Philippians 2:3–4.",
 "|In Philemon, Paul calls Onesimus his|brother|fellow worker|fellow prisoner|child": "Paul appeals for Onesimus as his child, whose father he became during imprisonment. He later urges Philemon to receive him as a beloved brother. The stem allows either description unless it specifies “my child” in verse 10, which is the wording behind the supplied key.\n\nBible reference: Philemon 10,16.",
 "|Believers awaiting the Lord should put on the|armour of hope|helmet of righteousness|breastplate of faith|breastplate of salvation": "Paul names a breastplate of faith and love and a helmet of the hope of salvation. The breastplate-of-faith option gives part of the correct pairing. The trap: the helmet and breastplate descriptions must not be exchanged, even though similar armour language occurs in Ephesians.\n\nBible reference: 1 Thessalonians 5:8.",
 "|According to 1 Corinthians, the same Spirit gives different gifts, while the same Lord gives different|services|prophecies|workings|healings": "Paul distinguishes varieties of gifts with the same Spirit, varieties of service with the same Lord, and varieties of working with the same God. The three parallel clauses make service the answer here. Healing and prophecy are particular gifts, not the matching category in this sentence.\n\nBible reference: 1 Corinthians 12:4–6.",
 "|“Take heart, it is I; have no fear.” Jesus said this when|Mary Magdalene saw him at the tomb|he appeared to two disciples on the Emmaus road|he was walking on the sea at night|the women saw him after the resurrection": "The disciples were terrified on seeing Jesus walk on the sea and thought he was a ghost. He identified himself and reassured them. These words belong to the storm episode, not the later resurrection appearances at the tomb or on the Emmaus road.\n\nBible reference: Matthew 14:25–27; Mark 6:48–50.",
 "|On returning from their mission, the seventy reported that|even demons were subject to them|Samaritans accepted their message|they saw Satan fall like lightning|all their needs were fully met": "The seventy told Jesus that even demons submitted to them in his name. Jesus then said he had seen Satan fall like lightning. The trap: the report about demons came from the returning disciples, while the saying about Satan came from Jesus in reply.\n\nBible reference: Luke 10:17–18.",
 "|“Blessed is the kingdom of our father David that is coming!” The crowd said this during the|transfiguration|feeding of the four thousand|Passover feast|triumphal entry into Jerusalem": "As Jesus entered Jerusalem, the crowd spread garments and branches and acclaimed the coming kingdom of David. The declaration belongs to the triumphal entry. Its messianic language concerns the significance of Jesus' arrival, not a saying at the feeding miracle.\n\nBible reference: Mark 11:7–10.",
 "|After the healing at Bethzatha, the Jews sought all the more to kill Jesus because|they hated the healed man|they disliked his healing|he claimed that God his Father was still working|he healed on the Sabbath": "The Sabbath healing first provoked persecution. The text then says they sought all the more to kill him because he called God his own Father, making himself equal with God. The Father's work explains the escalation; the Sabbath alone describes the preceding stage of the dispute.\n\nBible reference: John 5:16–18.",
 "|“To you I will give all this authority and their glory…” This offer was made during Jesus'|ascension|triumphal entry|Sermon on the Mount|temptation": "The devil showed Jesus the kingdoms and offered their authority in exchange for worship. Jesus answered that only God should be worshipped and served. Identify the speaker as the tempter; this is not a promise Jesus made during his ministry or ascension.\n\nBible reference: Luke 4:5–8.",
 "|After Peter's great confession, Jesus charged the disciples to|tell nobody that he was the Christ|leave the crowd and rest|announce that heaven's kingdom had come|beware of the Pharisees' leaven": "After Peter confessed Jesus as the Christ, the Son of the living God, Jesus instructed the disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Christ. The command belongs to that stage of his ministry, before the later commission to proclaim the risen Lord.\n\nBible reference: Matthew 16:13–20.",
 "|“There is joy before the angels of God over one sinner who repents.” Jesus said this after the parable of the|dishonest steward|prodigal son|lost coin|lost sheep": "The woman finding her lost coin calls her friends to rejoice, and Jesus follows with the saying about joy before God's angels. The lost sheep has a closely related conclusion in verse 7, but the particular wording quoted here concludes the coin parable in verse 10.\n\nBible reference: Luke 15:8–10.",
 "|According to Luke, the leper Jesus healed was commanded to|tell people about the cleansing|make an offering for his cleansing|show himself to his friends|ask the priest what offering he should make": "Jesus told the healed man to show himself to the priest and offer what Moses had commanded for cleansing. He did not send him to enquire what the law required. The instruction to make the prescribed offering is therefore the correct available option.\n\nBible reference: Luke 5:12–14.",
 "|The angel told Zechariah that his son would|go before God in the spirit and power of Elijah|turn children's hearts to their fathers|make the disobedient repent|turn many Israelites to God": "The angel says John will turn many Israelites to the Lord and go before him in Elijah's spirit and power. He will also turn the disobedient to the wisdom of the righteous. Several options paraphrase parts of this promise, so a broad stem cannot make only the Elijah description true.\n\nBible reference: Luke 1:16–17.",
 "|Rahab's reward for hiding Joshua's spies was that|all the city's precious metals were given to her|Jericho was spared for her|she married a spy and became queen|she and her household were spared": "Joshua ordered the spies to bring Rahab and her family out before the destruction of the city. She was spared because she hid the messengers. The narrative does not say that Jericho itself was spared or that she became a queen by marrying a spy.\n\nBible reference: Joshua 6:22–25.",
 "|“It is not good that the man should be alone…” What did God do next in the narrative?|Form a woman from a rib|Remove one of the man's ribs|Form beasts and birds and bring them to the man|Cause a deep sleep to fall upon the man": "After announcing the need for a suitable helper, Genesis describes the animals being formed and brought for naming. When no suitable helper is found, the deep sleep and formation of the woman follow. The question tests the narrative sequence, not simply the eventual answer to the man's solitude.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 2:18–22.",
 "|The people were to gather a day's portion of manna so that God could test their|patience|self-control|faith|obedience": "God said he would test whether the people would walk in his law by giving instructions about gathering manna. Daily dependence matters, but the explicit test concerns obeying his directions. The special provision before the Sabbath further tests attention to those commands.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 16:4.",
 "|Saul would not execute those who opposed his accession because|they had been reconciled to him|God had brought deliverance to Israel|God had forgiven their intentions|they had fought bravely": "After the victory over the Ammonites, some people wanted Saul's former opponents killed. Saul refused, declaring that the LORD had worked salvation in Israel that day. His response placed celebration of God's deliverance above personal revenge.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 11:12–13.",
 "|When Saul received no answer from God, he sought help from|a medium|Achish|Samuel|a prophet": "Saul asked his servants to find a woman who was a medium and went to her at Endor. He hoped to consult Samuel through her, but the person he sought out was the medium. Distinguish his immediate intermediary from the dead prophet he wanted summoned.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 28:6–8.",
 "|Saul promised whoever defeated Goliath|part of his land|his daughter in marriage|the position of second-in-command|command of his army": "The reported reward included great riches, marriage to the king's daughter and freedom for the victor's father's house. The option about Saul's daughter is one of these promises. Becoming commander or second-in-command is not part of the reward stated in this report.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 17:25.",
 "|After Samuel reported God's judgement on his household, Eli said|“It is the LORD; let him do what seems good to him”|he would rebuke his sons|sacrifice would be offered for them|God should have mercy on them": "Eli urged Samuel to report the whole message and then submitted to the LORD's judgement. His actual response acknowledges God's authority. The question asks what he said on hearing this message, not what better parental discipline might previously have prevented.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 3:15–18.",
 "|Before meeting God at Sinai, the Israelites were to|touch the mountain's border|be consecrated and wash their garments|fast and wash their feet|fast, pray and be consecrated": "God instructed Moses to consecrate the people and have them wash their clothes in preparation. Boundaries were set around the mountain, and touching it was forbidden. The trap: touching the mountain was a prohibited action, not part of the preparation.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 19:10–15.",
 "|One of Solomon's unwise policies was|using forced labour to build the Temple|making treaties with neighbouring kings|continually fighting wars|sacrificing at high places": "Solomon's labour demands formed part of the heavy yoke the people later asked Rehoboam to lighten. This explains the political-policy answer in the key. Sacrifice at high places is a religious problem discussed elsewhere, so the distinction is between burdensome state policy and religious conduct.\n\nBible reference: 1 Kings 5:13–18; 12:3–4.",
 "|God avoided the shorter route through Philistine territory because|he wanted Israel to learn through hardship|Egypt would overtake them|they feared the Philistines|seeing war might make them return to Egypt": "God did not lead Israel by the nearer Philistine road lest the people change their minds when they saw war and return to Egypt. The text supplies that specific reason. General lessons about hardship should not replace the explanation actually given for choosing the wilderness route.\n\nBible reference: Exodus 13:17–18.",
 "|Joseph's brothers decided not to kill him because he|pleaded with them|was their brother and their flesh|was loved by their father|was his mother's first son": "Judah proposed selling Joseph instead of killing him, saying that he was their brother and their flesh. Their decision spared his life but did not make selling him right. The quoted appeal to kinship, rather than Jacob's favouritism, explains Judah's proposal.\n\nBible reference: Genesis 37:26–27.",
 "|David's victory over the Amalekites who raided Ziklag followed|the size of his army|his oath not to harm an Egyptian servant|his seeking God's approval before pursuit|his personal military greatness": "David enquired of the LORD about pursuing the raiders and received assurance of recovery. The Egyptian servant later supplied important guidance, but the narrative grounds the successful pursuit in God's answer. The victory is not explained merely by David's strength or army size.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 30:7–8,16–20.",
 "|“Have this mind among yourselves, which is yours in Christ Jesus…” The mind is one of|faith|humility|prayer|holiness": "Paul urges believers not to act from selfish ambition, then presents Christ's self-humbling as their pattern. The “mind” is the attitude of humble, self-giving service. Faith, prayer and holiness matter elsewhere, but humility connects the instruction with the example that follows.\n\nBible reference: Philippians 2:3–8.",
 "|As one man's trespass brought condemnation, one man's righteous act brings|sanctification and life|salvation and new life|justification and hope|acquittal and life": "Paul contrasts Adam's trespass and condemnation with Christ's righteous act and justification leading to life. “Acquittal and life” expresses that legal-and-life contrast. The question tests the paired terms in this sentence rather than every benefit associated with salvation.\n\nBible reference: Romans 5:18.",
 "|Peter says servants should respect not only kind and gentle masters but also the|impatient|overbearing|inconsiderate|wicked": "The passage speaks of submitting not only to the good and gentle but also to the harsh or overbearing, called “froward” in the KJV. The point concerns difficult treatment rather than mere thoughtlessness. This ancient household instruction does not make a master's abuse morally acceptable.\n\nBible reference: 1 Peter 2:18.",
 "|Paul says Christians do not all have the same function, but their gifts differ according to|devotion to prayer|enablement by the Spirit|suffering and endurance|the grace given to them": "Romans compares believers with different members of one body and says their gifts differ according to the grace given to them. The printed stem wrongly says they have the same functions. Gifts involve the Spirit elsewhere, but this sentence specifically names grace.\n\nBible reference: Romans 12:4–6.",
 "|Those baptised into Christ are baptised into his|kingdom|service|death|suffering": "Paul asks whether believers know that baptism into Christ Jesus is baptism into his death. He develops the image of burial and resurrection to explain new life. Service and kingdom membership may relate to Christian identity, but they are not the word completing this statement.\n\nBible reference: Romans 6:3–4.",
 "|“Let steadfastness have its full effect.” James says the result should be|righteousness and faith|perfection and completeness|trial and endurance|humility and obedience": "James says that tested faith produces steadfastness and that steadfastness should finish its work so believers become mature and complete, lacking nothing. Trials are the occasion for this growth, not the final outcome named in the sentence.\n\nBible reference: James 1:2–4.",
 "|According to Paul, just as the law came to increase trespass, sin came to increase|ungodliness|punishment|righteousness|grace": "Paul's actual wording is that where sin increased, grace abounded all the more. The printed stem loosely makes this sound as though sin exists to produce grace. The intended answer is grace, but Paul immediately rejects continuing in sin so that grace may abound.\n\nBible reference: Romans 5:20–6:2.",
 "|According to Peter, Christians who suffer according to God's will will receive|forgiveness|blessing|the crown of glory|the Holy Spirit": "Peter calls those suffering for righteousness blessed and speaks of joy when Christ's glory is revealed. He specifically promises an unfading crown of glory to faithful elders. The stem does not identify which passage it means, so blessing is also defensible alongside the key's crown-of-glory answer.\n\nBible reference: 1 Peter 3:14; 4:13–19; 5:4.",
 "|Romans tells Christians to put on the Lord Jesus Christ and not|make provision for the flesh|rely on the law|judge one another|take advantage of one another": "Paul contrasts decent conduct with revelry, drunkenness, sexual immorality, quarrelling and jealousy, then urges believers not to provide for the flesh's desires. “Put on Christ” therefore concerns a changed way of living, not merely adopting a religious description of oneself.\n\nBible reference: Romans 13:13–14.",
 "|Elijah decreed that there would be neither rain nor dew for three years because|Nebat son of Rehoboam plotted with Ahab against him|Ahab forsook God's command by erecting an altar for Baal|Jezebel killed Obadiah|he knew ravens would feed him": "Ahab established Baal worship, and Elijah later identified abandonment of God's commands and following the Baals as the source of Israel's trouble. The drought challenges that unfaithfulness. The ravens were God's provision for Elijah afterwards, not the moral reason for announcing judgement. The printed duration is simplified: Kings mentions a return in the third year, while Luke 4:25 and James 5:17 describe three years and six months.\n\nBible reference: 1 Kings 16:30–17:1; 18:18.",
 "|After Naboth's murder, God declared that|he would require Naboth's blood from Ahab's hand|Ahab's descendants would never sit on Israel's throne|dogs would lick Ahab's blood where they had licked Naboth's|the sword would never leave Ahab's house": "Elijah announced a judgement matching Ahab's seizure of Naboth's vineyard after his death: dogs would lick Ahab's blood. The other statements either generalise judgement or echo a different royal story. Identify the concrete sentence spoken in this confrontation.\n\nBible reference: 1 Kings 21:17–19.",
 "|One sin of Manasseh linked to Judah's judgement was|releasing a thousand captives|shedding innocent blood|failing to obey prophets|arresting Babylon's king": "Kings links the raiding bands and Judah's removal with Manasseh's sins, particularly the innocent blood with which he filled Jerusalem. This is the specific wrongdoing singled out in the passage. It is not an account of Manasseh capturing the Babylonian king.\n\nBible reference: 2 Kings 24:2–4.",
 "|Hosea urged returning Israel to ask God to|take away their iniquity|accept their worship|bless them abundantly|give them victory in battle": "Hosea tells Israel to return with words and ask God to take away all iniquity and accept what is good. The prayer includes renouncing false reliance on military strength and idols. The exact request concerns forgiveness, not a generic appeal for successful battles.\n\nBible reference: Hosea 14:1–3.",
 "|Jeremiah says nations will bless themselves in God and glory in him when Israel|comes with prayer and fasting|returns and glorifies him|becomes loyal and repents|swears in truth, justice and uprightness": "Jeremiah calls Israel to return, put away abominations and swear “As the LORD lives” in truth, justice and righteousness. He then speaks of nations blessing themselves and glorying in God. The final option follows the particular terms attached to that consequence.\n\nBible reference: Jeremiah 4:1–2.",
 "|Josiah ordered an enquiry about the discovered law book because|he realised God was angry with Israel|people demanded its interpretation|he was excited by its discovery|nobody could read it": "After hearing the book, Josiah tore his clothes and said the LORD's wrath was great because previous generations had not obeyed its words. He sought the LORD through an enquiry. The book had already been read aloud, so inability to read it cannot explain his command.\n\nBible reference: 2 Kings 22:11–13.",
 "|Amos says God would raise an oppressive nation against Israel because the people|were corrupt and enslaved the poor|rejected God and sold the righteous for silver|turned justice into poison|boasted of achievements by their own strength": "The threat to raise a nation in Amos 6:14 immediately follows turning justice into poison and boasting of strength in verses 12–13. Both nearby charges fit. Selling the righteous for silver is a genuine charge in Amos 2:6, but it is not the immediate context of this threat.\n\nBible reference: Amos 6:12–14; 2:6.",
 "|On the third day after capturing the ark, the Philistines found that|Dagon had disappeared|Dagon had fallen and broken to pieces|the ark had swallowed Dagon|the ark was standing on Dagon": "Dagon first fell before the ark and was put back. The next morning his head and hands lay cut off on the threshold. The story contrasts the apparent victory of capturing Israel's ark with the powerlessness of the Philistine idol.\n\nBible reference: 1 Samuel 5:2–5.",
 "|“Before I formed you in the womb I knew you…” God said this at|Ezekiel's call|Jeremiah's confinement in a cistern|Isaiah's consecration|Jeremiah's call": "God's words introduce Jeremiah's call as a prophet to the nations. Jeremiah objects that he is young, and God promises his presence and places his words in his mouth. The statement does not belong to the later account of Jeremiah being lowered into a cistern.\n\nBible reference: Jeremiah 1:4–10.",
 "|What did Josiah do to Jeroboam's altar at Bethel?|Remove its objects|Break its stone into pieces|Burn it down|Pronounce curses on it": "Second Kings 23:15 says Josiah demolished the altar and high place, then describes burning the high place and reducing it to dust. B best describes the action on the altar. The burning option refers to an associated action on the high place; keep the objects distinct rather than accepting both automatically.\n\nBible reference: 2 Kings 23:15.",
 "|God called Jonah and sent him to Nineveh to|pray for the people's forgiveness|pray for their repentance|preach his word to them|cry against their wickedness": "The first commission was to cry against Nineveh because its wickedness had come before God. The renewed commission in chapter 3 tells Jonah to proclaim God's message. Both crying against wickedness and preaching God's word describe his mission, though the first wording matches the opening call.\n\nBible reference: Jonah 1:1–2; 3:1–2.",
 "|“We must work the works of him who sent me while it is day…” Jesus said this in the episode of healing the|man born blind|centurion's servant|ten lepers|paralysed man": "Jesus spoke about doing the sender's works before making mud, anointing the blind man's eyes and sending him to wash. The supplied paper says the saying came after the healing, but the sequence in John is before. The person involved is still the man born blind.\n\nBible reference: John 9:1–7.",
 "|“If this undertaking is of men it will fail; but if it is of God, you cannot overthrow them…” Gamaliel referred to|Paul and Timothy|Peter and John|Peter and the apostles|Paul and Silas": "Gamaliel counselled restraint when the apostles stood before the council after their renewed arrest. Acts introduces their reply as that of Peter and the apostles. Peter and John were prominent in the earlier council hearing, but this later episode concerns the larger apostolic group.\n\nBible reference: Acts 5:27–39.",
 "|Jesus said people loved darkness rather than light because|their hearts were hardened|their deeds were evil|they had no truth|they were blind": "John connects avoidance of the light with evil deeds that people do not want exposed. Those who do what is true come to the light. The contrast concerns moral response to God's revelation, not a statement that physical blindness causes unbelief.\n\nBible reference: John 3:19–21.",
 "|Ananias and Sapphira's deaths caused|great fear among the disciples|greater respect for Peter|many immediate conversions|greater unity": "After both deaths, great fear came upon the whole church and everyone who heard. Their offence involved deceit about the gift, not a rule requiring every believer to sell all property. The question asks for the effect expressly reported at the story's close.\n\nBible reference: Acts 5:1–11.",
 "|“They have taken the Lord out of the tomb…” What immediately followed Mary's report?|The guards fled|Peter and the other disciple went towards the tomb|Two angels appeared to Mary|Mary stood weeping outside": "Mary first reported the missing body to Peter and the other disciple, who then went towards the tomb. Her encounter with two angels occurs later. The trap: both events are in John 20, but the word “immediately” requires the first response to her report.\n\nBible reference: John 20:1–12.",
 "|Paul and Barnabas were sent from Antioch towards Seleucia by the|Holy Spirit|church elders|other disciples|prophets and teachers": "During worship and fasting, the Holy Spirit directed the church to set apart Barnabas and Saul. The leaders prayed, laid hands on them and released them, but Acts explicitly calls them sent out by the Holy Spirit. Distinguish divine initiative from the church's commissioning actions.\n\nBible reference: Acts 13:1–4.",
 "|“Are you the only visitor to Jerusalem who does not know what happened?” The Emmaus disciples referred to Jesus'|triumphal entry and cleansing of the Temple|arrest, trials and judgement|crucifixion, death and resurrection|birth and presentation": "Cleopas recounted Jesus' condemnation and crucifixion and the reports of the empty tomb and that he was alive. The disciples had not yet recognised their companion as the risen Jesus. The option about death and resurrection best includes both their grief and the day's astonishing reports.\n\nBible reference: Luke 24:18–24.",
 "|“This Jesus God raised up, and of that we are all witnesses.” Peter said this during|the healing at the Beautiful Gate|his release from prison|the choice of Judas' replacement|his Pentecost sermon": "In the Pentecost sermon, Peter explains the Spirit's coming by proclaiming Jesus' death, resurrection and exaltation. The quoted sentence is Acts 2:32. Peter also witnesses to resurrection elsewhere, but this wording and setting belong to Pentecost.\n\nBible reference: Acts 2:32.",
 "|According to John, a person who does not believe in God's only Son is|none of his own|cut off as a branch|condemned already|unworthy of the kingdom": "John contrasts the believer, who is not condemned, with the unbeliever, who is condemned already because of rejecting God's only Son. The branch imagery appears in John 15, a different discourse. Answer from the judgement-and-belief statement in chapter 3.\n\nBible reference: John 3:18."
};
